// Stories for the matrix: STORIES["<civ id>:<activity>"] = "…". See cultures/README.md.
Object.assign(STORIES, {
  // ── Hindustanis ──
  "hindustanis:hunt": "In the Mughal مغل shikargah شکارگاہ the emperor Akbar اکبر ordered the qamargha قمرغہ, a great ring hunt: thousands of beaters closed a circle many miles wide over days, driving deer, nilgai and leopards toward the centre. In 1578 near Bhera, at the height of such a hunt, Akbar suddenly halted it, freed the animals and sat in silence under a tree. Courtiers called it a spiritual seizure. The hunt was power; the release was something deeper.",
  "hindustanis:battle": "Panipat पानीपत, 21 April 1526. Babur بابر, with barely twelve thousand men, lashed carts together with chains and set his matchlocks and cannon behind them. Ibrahim Lodi ابراہیم لودی came on with tens of thousands and a thousand elephants. The guns roared, the elephants panicked and trampled their own ranks, and by noon the sultan lay dead on the field. Babur wrote in his memoir that God had made the hard easy. A new empire, the Mughal مغل, began in smoke.",
  "hindustanis:gather": "Imagined: in the Doab दोआब between the Ganga गंगा and the Yamuna यमुना, women of a village go out at dawn after the rabi harvest to glean wheat ears and gather mahua महुआ flowers fallen in the night. Baskets on their heads, they sing kajri कजरी and bicker over who found the sweetest tree. The mahua will be dried, cooked, brewed. Little is wasted; the land between two rivers has fed them for three thousand years, and they expect it to keep doing so.",
  "hindustanis:ritual": "At Varanasi वाराणसी the pandas sit on the ghats beside the Ganga गंगा with palm-leaf almanacs, reading the stars for those who come. A merchant asks about a journey; a father about his daughter's marriage. The priest consults the nakshatra नक्षत्र, names an auspicious muhurat मुहूर्त, and tells him to offer flowers and a lamp to the river. As dusk falls the Ganga aarti आरती begins, brass lamps swung in great arcs. No one is sad here; the future is being asked for, politely.",
  "hindustanis:rain": "By June the plains of Hindustan हिंदुस्तान bake at forty-five degrees; wells sink, cattle stand panting under neem trees. Everyone watches the southwest sky. Then the first clouds of the monsoon pile up, the smell of wet earth, mitti ki khushboo मिट्टी की ख़ुशबू, rises before the rain itself, and the downpour hits. Children dance in the lanes, women hang swings from mango trees for the festival of Teej तीज and sing sawan सावन songs. The whole country exhales at once.",
  "hindustanis:flood": "The Yamuna यमुना and the Ganga गंगा do not stay in their beds. In monsoon years the rivers spread for miles across the Doab दोआब, carrying away mud houses and leaving villages as islands. Families climb onto roofs with their goats and grain, waiting for the water to fall. Yet the farmers know the flood brings new silt; the field drowned in Bhadon भादों will give a rich crop in winter. They mourn the loss and count the gift together.",
  "hindustanis:wind": "Before the monsoon comes the andhi आँधी, a dust storm that rolls across the Gangetic plain as a brown wall hundreds of metres high. The sky turns copper, then dark at midday. Shopkeepers in Delhi दिल्ली and Agra आगरा slam their shutters, camels kneel and close their eyes, and the dust finds its way into every jar and fold of cloth. When it passes, the air is cooler, and people say the rain is near now.",
  "hindustanis:fire": "At Manikarnika मणिकर्णिका ghat in Varanasi वाराणसी the pyres on the bank of the Ganga गंगा are never allowed to go out. The Dom डोम keepers of the ghat tend a fire said to have burned for thousands of years, and every pyre is lit from it. Day and night the bodies come down the lanes on bamboo biers, wood is weighed on great scales, and smoke drifts over the river. Here fire is not a disaster but a release: to be burned at Kashi काशी is to be freed.",
  "hindustanis:thunder": "In the songs of Hindustan हिंदुस्तान, thunder is the voice of Indra इन्द्र riding his elephant Airavata ऐरावत through the clouds, hurling the vajra वज्र. When the first thunder of the monsoon cracks over the plains, peacocks scream in answer and lovers apart are said to ache, a theme poets from Kalidasa कालिदास to Tansen तानसेन returned to again and again. The raga Megh Malhar मेघ मल्हार, it was said, could call down rain itself. The thunder is both fear and promise.",
  "hindustanis:birth": "In a Mughal مغل palace at Fatehpur Sikri फ़तेहपुर सीकरी in 1569, the son long prayed for was born to Akbar اکبر after he walked barefoot to the Sufi Salim Chishti سلیم چشتی for a blessing. He named the boy Salim; he would become Jahangir جہانگیر. In village homes too, a birth is announced with a brass plate beaten and sweets handed out, and the elders wonder aloud what this child will be. Mothers die easily; hope runs anyway.",
  "hindustanis:death": "In Hindustan हिंदुस्तान a death is met quickly. The body is washed, wrapped in white, laid on a bamboo bier, and carried to the burning ghat while the men chant Ram naam satya hai राम नाम सत्य है, the name of God is truth. The eldest son lights the pyre and later cracks the skull so the soul may leave. Thirteen days of mourning follow, and the ashes go to the Ganga गंगा, the river that carries everyone home.",

  // ── Indians ──
  "indians:hunt": "Imagined: in a Rajput राजपूत court of Mewar मेवाड़, the prince rides out for shikar शिकार with cheetahs hooded on bullock carts, as painters of the 18th century often showed. When blackbuck are sighted, the hood is lifted and the cheetah streaks across the plain. The hunt is a lesson in courage and patience, and afterward court artists record it in bright miniatures. For the Rajputs राजपूत, the jungle was a school for war.",
  "indians:battle": "Kalinga कलिंग, around 261 BCE. The Maurya मौर्य emperor Ashoka 𑀅𑀲𑁄𑀓 / अशोक conquered the kingdom on the eastern coast in a war so bloody that his own edicts record a hundred thousand slain and a hundred and fifty thousand carried away. Then Ashoka did something no conqueror had done: he publicly confessed remorse, carving it in stone, and turned to the dhamma धम्म of the Buddha. The battle he won became the battle he regretted for the rest of his life.",
  "indians:gather": "Imagined: in the Western Ghats, women of a forest village gather honey, wild pepper and cardamom pods, singing to each other so as not to surprise an elephant. The pepper vine climbs every tree; for two thousand years it has drawn Roman, Arab and Portuguese ships to the Malabar മലബാർ coast. These women do not think of empires. They think of the season, the bees, and getting home before the rain.",
  "indians:ritual": "Vedic sacrifice: priests build the fire altar of bricks laid in exact geometry, the shulba sutras शुल्बसूत्र giving its measurements. The hotr होतृ chants the hymns of the Rig Veda ऋग्वेद, the adhvaryu अध्वर्यु pours ghee into Agni's flames, and the fire carries the offering to the gods. Later, astrologers drew up a child's horoscope from the jyotisha ज्योतिष texts to ask what lay ahead. The ritual is precise, solemn, and confident: the cosmos listens to correct words.",
  "indians:rain": "On the Kerala കേരളം coast around the first of June, the whole of India waits for the monsoon to break, and the weather office still announces it like a national event. Farmers in Maharashtra and Punjab watch the forecasts; if it is late, prices rise and prayers rise with them. When it comes, the land turns green in a week, schoolchildren float paper boats, and the country, in a sense, begins its year again.",
  "indians:flood": "Imagined: on the Brahmaputra ব্ৰহ্মপুত্ৰ in Assam অসম the river swells every monsoon until it is miles wide. The Mising people build their houses on stilts, chang ghar চাং ঘৰ, and keep boats tied to the doorposts. When the water rises they simply move upstairs with the hens and wait. Some years the flood takes everything; other years it only visits. They live with the river, not against it, as their grandparents did.",
  "indians:wind": "In October 1999 a super cyclone struck the coast of Odisha ଓଡ଼ିଶା with winds above 250 km/h and a storm surge that drove seawater miles inland. Around ten thousand people died. Odisha remembered. When Cyclone Phailin came in 2013, the state evacuated nearly a million people into shelters, and the death toll fell to a few dozen. Grief had been turned into preparation, and the coast counts that as a victory.",
  "indians:fire": "Holika Dahan होलिका दहन: on the night before Holi होली, villages and city lanes across India light great bonfires of wood and cow-dung cakes. The story tells of the demoness Holika who sat in fire holding the boy Prahlad प्रह्लाद, but the fire spared the devotee and burned her. People circle the flames, roast fresh grain, and the next morning throw coloured powder at one another. Here fire is not destruction but cleansing.",
  "indians:thunder": "Indra इन्द्र, king of the gods in the Rig Veda ऋग्वेद, slew the serpent Vritra वृत्र with his thunderbolt and freed the waters to flow. Nearly a quarter of the hymns are addressed to him. When thunder rolls over the plains, the old meaning still sits behind it: the drought-demon has been struck and the rivers are released. Farmers look up at the sound and smile, because thunder means the waters are coming.",
  "indians:birth": "The samskaras संस्कार begin before birth with ceremonies for the mother. When the child arrives, the father whispers into its ear and touches honey and ghee to its lips in the jatakarma जातकर्म rite; on the twelfth day comes the namakarana नामकरण, the naming. The astrologer casts the horoscope and everyone leans in to hear it. Childbirth was dangerous and many did not survive, but every newborn might become a king, a saint, or a poet.",
  "indians:death": "The antyesti अन्त्येष्टि, the last sacrifice. The body is bathed and dressed, a lamp is lit by its head, and the family carries it to the cremation ground, the shmashana श्मशान. The eldest son circles the pyre with fire and lights it. On the third day the bones are collected; on the tenth or thirteenth the shraddha श्राद्ध rites feed the departed soul. Grief is real, but the teaching is calm: the self does not die; it only changes clothes.",

  // ── Bengalis ──
  "bengalis:hunt": "In the Sundarbans সুন্দরবন, the mangrove forest of the Ganges delta, honey-gatherers called mouals মৌয়াল and woodcutters go in by boat, and the tigers hunt them. Before entering they pray to Bonbibi বনবিবি, the forest goddess honoured by Hindus and Muslims alike, who protects the poor against the tiger-demon Dakshin Rai দক্ষিণ রায়. The men wear masks on the backs of their heads to confuse the tigers. Here the hunter is often the hunted.",
  "bengalis:battle": "Plassey পলাশী, 23 June 1757. Siraj ud-Daulah সিরাজউদ্দৌলা, the young Nawab of Bengal, met Robert Clive's small East India Company force in a mango grove on the Bhagirathi ভাগীরথী. A rainstorm soaked the Nawab's powder while the British kept theirs dry under tarpaulins, and Mir Jafar মীর জাফর, his commander, held his troops back by secret agreement. The Nawab fled and was killed days later. Bengal remembers the betrayal more than the battle.",
  "bengalis:gather": "Imagined: in the low country of Bengal after the aman আমন rice harvest, families wade into the ponds to gather shapla শাপলা water lilies and catch small fish by hand, while children collect date-palm sap for jaggery in the cold mornings. Nobanna নবান্ন, the festival of new rice, follows with pitha পিঠা cakes and songs. In a land of water, gathering means a basket, a net and wet ankles, and the mood is generous.",
  "bengalis:ritual": "Durga Puja দুর্গাপূজা in autumn: for five days Bengal worships the goddess Durga, who slew the buffalo-demon Mahishasura মহিষাসুর. Clay images, shaped by potters of Kumartuli কুমোরটুলি in Kolkata কলকাতা, stand in pandals. Drummers play the dhak ঢাক, women offer lamps, and the priest chants Sanskrit verses. People ask the goddess for protection in the year ahead. On the last day she is carried to the river and immersed, and the crowds say: come again next year.",
  "bengalis:rain": "Rabindranath Tagore রবীন্দ্রনাথ ঠাকুর wrote dozens of songs for the barsha বর্ষা, the monsoon, and Bengal still sings them when the clouds come. After the furnace of Boishakh বৈশাখ, the first heavy rain falls on tin roofs and banana leaves, the ponds fill, frogs start up their chorus, and fishermen grin. Poets sit by windows with tea and muri মুড়ি. In Bengal the rains are not just weather; they are the season of longing and of song.",
  "bengalis:flood": "In 1998 two-thirds of Bangladesh lay underwater for more than two months as the Ganges, Brahmaputra and Meghna মেঘনা rose together. Millions lost homes and crops. Yet the delta itself is built by floods: every year the rivers lay down fresh silt that keeps Bengal among the most fertile places on earth. Villagers raise their homes on earthen plinths and keep a boat ready. Their word for the good flood, barsha বর্ষা, differs from the bad one, bonna বন্যা.",
  "bengalis:wind": "On the night of 12 November 1970 the Bhola ভোলা cyclone struck the delta with a storm surge that swept over the low islands while families slept. Perhaps three hundred thousand people died, the deadliest tropical cyclone ever recorded. Fishermen who survived clung to palm trees until dawn. In the years after, villagers built raised cyclone shelters and trained volunteers who cycle through the islands with megaphones and flags when a storm comes. The delta grieves, and prepares.",
  "bengalis:fire": "Imagined: in a village near Bishnupur বিষ্ণুপুর, the terracotta temple town, potters fire their kilns at night before the Durga Puja দুর্গাপূজা season. The whole family tends the flames, feeding straw and dung cakes, and children are told stories of the gods while the clay hardens into horses and goddesses. A cracked pot is a small grief; a good firing means food for a month. The fire glows red over the fields.",
  "bengalis:thunder": "Kalbaishakhi কালবৈশাখী: the nor'westers of spring. In the afternoons of April and May, black clouds rise suddenly in the northwest, and within minutes a storm of thunder, lightning and hail tears across Bengal. Mango crops are lost, boats overturned on the rivers, and lightning kills farmers in the open fields. Yet the storms also break the heat, and Bengalis welcome them with a mix of fear and relief, as the year's wild herald.",
  "bengalis:birth": "Imagined: in a village in the delta, the women ring the conch three times and ululate, ulu dhvani উলুধ্বনি, to announce that a child has been born. The midwife, the dai দাই, has done this a hundred times and lost some. The grandmother marks the baby with a smudge of kajal কাজল to keep away the evil eye. On the sixth night, Shashthi ষষ্ঠী, the goddess of children, is honoured, for the fate of the child is said to be written that night.",
  "bengalis:death": "At Nimtala নিমতলা ghat in Kolkata কলকাতা on the Hooghly হুগলি, the dead have been burned for centuries; Tagore ঠাকুর himself was cremated there in 1941 amid vast crowds. Mourners chant Bolo Hari, Hari bol বলো হরি, হরি বোল, as they carry the bier through the streets. The son performs mukhagni মুখাগ্নি, touching fire to the mouth, and the river receives the ashes. Bengali Muslims, meanwhile, bury their dead quickly beneath a simple mound, and recite the janaza জানাজা prayer.",

  // ── Dravidians ──
  "dravidians:hunt": "The Sangam poems of old Tamilakam தமிழகம், written in the first centuries CE, divide the land into five tinai திணை. In kurinji குறிஞ்சி, the hills, men hunted boar and elephant with bows and trapped deer, while girls guarded the millet fields from parrots with slings and rattles. Lovers met in the hills by night. The hunters sang to Murugan முருகன், the young red god of the mountains, and offered him a share of the kill.",
  "dravidians:battle": "Imagined, after the Sangam poems: before a war, Tamil warriors of the Chera சேரர், Chola சோழர் and Pandya பாண்டியர் kings drank toddy, raised the war drum, and swore loyalty to their lord. Those who fell were honoured with a nadukal நடுகல், a hero stone set up on the field, carved with their image, and worshipped by their village. Death in battle was not a waste but the finest end a man could have, said the puram poems.",
  "dravidians:gather": "On the Coromandel coast at Kayalpattinam காயல்பட்டினம் and Tuticorin தூத்துக்குடி, pearl divers of the Paravar பரவர் community dove for oysters in the Gulf of Mannar, a fishery known to Greek and Roman merchants. Holding a stone to sink fast, they went down without breath for a minute at a time, while shark-charmers chanted on the boats. Most oysters held nothing. A few held pearls that ended up in the crowns of kings across the world.",
  "dravidians:ritual": "At the great Brihadeeswara temple in Thanjavur தஞ்சாவூர், built by Rajaraja Chola இராசராச சோழன் around 1010, hundreds of temple servants, musicians and dancers tended the god Shiva சிவன். Inscriptions name them. Before a voyage or a war, the king made offerings; priests bathed the lingam in milk and honey, lamps burned through the night, and the oduvars ஓதுவார் sang the Tevaram தேவாரம் hymns. The Cholas asked the god for victory, and their fleets reached Srivijaya.",
  "dravidians:rain": "Tamil Nadu does not get its main rains from the summer monsoon but from the northeast monsoon of October and November. Through the long dry summer the farmers watch their tanks, the eri ஏரி, shrink to cracked mud. When the rains finally come, the tanks fill, the rice is sown, and the old song goes up: vaan sirappu வான் சிறப்பு, the excellence of rain, as Tiruvalluvar திருவள்ளுவர் wrote: without rain, even the sea would fail.",
  "dravidians:flood": "Aadi Perukku ஆடிப்பெருக்கு: in the Tamil month of Aadi, the Kaveri காவிரி swells with monsoon water from the western hills and floods down through the Chola சோழர் delta. On the eighteenth day, families gather on its banks to thank the river, offering rice, flowers, and yellow threads, and newly married women float lamps on the water. The Kallanai கல்லணை, the Grand Anicut built by Karikala Chola கரிகால் சோழன் around the 2nd century, still turns this flood into fields.",
  "dravidians:wind": "Imagined, after the old story: Tamil tradition remembers the kadal kol கடல்கோள், the swallowing by the sea, when a great flood took the land of the first Sangam far to the south. Fishing villages on the Coromandel coast still fear the storms that come in from the Bay of Bengal each November. The women wait on the shore for the catamarans; the men return soaked, laughing, or not at all.",
  "dravidians:fire": "Theemithi தீமிதி, the fire walk: in villages of Tamil Nadu, devotees of the goddess Draupadi Amman திரௌபதி அம்மன் walk barefoot across a long pit of glowing coals, after weeks of fasting and the recitation of the Mahabharata. Drums beat, the crowd shouts, and the walkers go one by one, faces calm. The fire honours Draupadi's proof of her purity. To walk it unhurt is a sign that the goddess has heard the vow.",
  "dravidians:thunder": "Imagined: in the Kerala കേരളം monsoon, thunderstorms come down from the Western Ghats in the evenings, shaking the coconut palms. Grandmothers in the tharavad തറവാട് house light the nilavilakku നിലവിളക്ക് lamp and chant Arjuna's ten names, believed to ward off lightning. Children are hushed and pulled away from the windows. Then the rain pours on the tiled roofs and the old women go back to their stories.",
  "dravidians:birth": "Imagined: in a Tamil house, a pregnant woman goes home to her mother's family for the valaikaapu வளைகாப்பு ceremony, where her arms are filled with glass bangles whose jingling is said to soothe the unborn child. When labour comes, the women of the family surround her. The newborn's name will often honour a grandparent; whether the child will be a farmer, a poet like Avvaiyar ஔவையார், or a soldier, only time can tell.",
  "dravidians:death": "Imagined: in a South Indian village, the paraiyar drummers lead the funeral procession, the thudumbu துடும்பு and parai பறை beating loud and fast through the streets, for the drum was believed to open the way for the soul. Women beat their breasts and sing oppari ஒப்பாரி laments praising the dead. Among some castes the body is buried seated; among others it is burned. On the sixteenth day, the family eats together again.",

  // ── Gurjaras ──
  "gurjaras:hunt": "The Gurjara-Pratiharas गुर्जर-प्रतिहार, who ruled from Kannauj कन्नौज in the 9th century, were described by the Arab traveller Sulaiman as having the finest cavalry in India. Their princes trained for war through the hunt, riding down boar and antelope across the dry plains of Rajasthan राजस्थान and Gujarat ગુજરાત. Imagined: a young prince, spear raised, rides past thorny khejri खेजड़ी trees after a wild boar, his father watching to see if he flinches.",
  "gurjaras:battle": "Around 738 CE, Arab armies from Sindh pressed east into Gujarat ગુજરાત and Rajasthan राजस्थान. The Gurjara-Pratihara गुर्जर-प्रतिहार king Nagabhata नागभट I and allied rulers threw them back, and for nearly three centuries the Pratiharas held the western frontier. Arab chroniclers called their king the greatest foe of Islam in India. The Gurjaras saw themselves as the guardians of Bharat's gate, and their inscriptions boasted of it.",
  "gurjaras:gather": "Imagined: in the Thar थार, Gurjar herders roam with their buffalo, cows and camels. In the dry season the women gather ker berries and sangri सांगरी pods from the khejri खेजड़ी tree, sun-drying them for the year, and gum from the trees. The desert gives little, but the people know its every gift. They cook ker-sangri with yogurt and spices, a dish still eaten in Rajasthan राजस्थान today.",
  "gurjaras:ritual": "Imagined: at the sun temple of Modhera મોઢેરા, built by the Solanki સોલંકી kings of Gujarat ગુજરાત in 1026, priests wait for the equinox, when the first rays of dawn strike the image of Surya સૂર્ય in the inner shrine. The step-well tank before the temple is lined with a hundred small shrines. A king asks if his next campaign will succeed; the priests read the omens of the sunrise. They answer gravely, and the king bows.",
  "gurjaras:rain": "In the desert country of Rajasthan राजस्थान and Gujarat ગુજરાત, every drop of rain was stored. The great stepwells, like Rani ki Vav રાણકી વાવ built at Patan પાટણ by the Solanki સોલંકી queen Udayamati ઉદયમતી around 1063, descend seven storeys to the water. Through the dry months women walk down the carved steps to draw water. When the monsoon finally arrives, the wells fill, and people climb down to see the water rising past the goddesses carved in the walls.",
  "gurjaras:flood": "Imagined: in the Rann of Kutch કચ્છનું રણ, the monsoon floods the salt flats so that the white desert becomes a shallow sea for months. The herders move their camels and buffalo to the higher bets, the islands. Villages wait for the water to evaporate, when salt workers return to their pans. The flood is part of the year here, not a disaster, and the old people can read it from the sky.",
  "gurjaras:wind": "Imagined: in the Thar थार, the loo लू blows in May, a hot wind from the west that dries the throat and can kill. Herders shelter their animals in the lee of dunes and wrap their faces. Women keep water in clay pots, matkas मटके, to cool. The children are told not to go out in the midday wind. When the evening comes, the wind drops, and the whole village comes out into the cooling air.",
  "gurjaras:fire": "Imagined, after the Rajput tradition of jauhar जौहर: when the fort of Chittorgarh चित्तौड़गढ़ was besieged, as in 1303 and again later, the women, rather than be captured, entered a great fire together, led by the queen, while the men put on saffron robes and rode out to die. The stories are told to this day with pride and with horror. The fort still stands above the plain, quiet.",
  "gurjaras:thunder": "Imagined: on the Aravalli अरावली hills above Mount Abu, the monsoon thunder echoes against the granite cliffs. Below, at the Dilwara देलवाड़ा temples, built by Jain ministers of the Solanki સોલંકી kings, monks continue their recitation, unmoved. The villagers in the valley say the god of the mountain is speaking. The cows huddle together, and a herder boy sings to them loudly to cover the sound of the storm.",
  "gurjaras:birth": "Imagined: in a Gurjar herding family, the birth of a child is announced by the women singing at the doorway. A knife is placed by the mother's bed against evil spirits. On the sixth day, the family lights a lamp to Bemata बेमाता, the goddess who writes the child's fate on its forehead. Will this child lead the herds, or ride off to serve a king? The family prays the lines are kind.",
  "gurjaras:death": "Imagined: in a Gurjar village of Rajasthan राजस्थान, the body is washed and carried to the cremation ground beyond the fields. A hero who died defending the cattle may be honoured with a devli देवली, a memorial stone carved with a horseman. On certain days, his descendants pour milk on the stone and light a lamp. The dead are not gone; they stand guard over the herd forever.",

  // ── Delhi Sultanate ──
  "delhi-sultanate:hunt": "Sultan Balban بلبن, who ruled Delhi دہلی from 1266 to 1287, used great hunting expeditions as military exercise. He rode out with thousands of horsemen into the jungles around Delhi, which were also full of bandits, and cleared them as he hunted. Mongol envoys were said to be impressed. For Balban, the hunt was statecraft: a sultan must be seen to be fearless and his army always ready.",
  "delhi-sultanate:battle": "Tarain तराइन, 1192. A year after his defeat by Prithviraj Chauhan पृथ्वीराज चौहान on the same field, Muhammad of Ghor محمد غوری returned with mounted archers. He feigned retreat, wore down the Rajput army through the day, and then charged with his reserve cavalry. Prithviraj was captured and killed. Ghor's general Qutb ud-Din Aibak قطب‌الدین ایبک stayed on in Delhi دہلی, and in 1206 founded the sultanate. For the Rajputs, Tarain became a lament; for the Turks, a beginning.",
  "delhi-sultanate:gather": "Imagined: in the markets of Delhi دہلی under Alauddin Khilji علاءالدین خلجی, who fixed prices around 1300, grain merchants bring wheat and rice to the government stores. Spies report any cheating, and short weight is punished harshly. Farmers bring their harvest to the city gates; women gather herbs and dried dung from the fields. Everyone eats cheaply, but nobody dares to complain aloud.",
  "delhi-sultanate:ritual": "Imagined: before a campaign, the sultan does not consult astrologers; instead he performs istikhara استخاره, two units of prayer followed by a supplication asking God to guide him to what is good. He sleeps and waits for clarity. In the mosque of Quwwat ul-Islam قوة الاسلام, beneath the Qutb Minar قطب منار, the ulema علما read the Quran and pray for the army. The mood is grave and quiet: the outcome belongs to God alone.",
  "delhi-sultanate:rain": "Imagined: the Hauz-i-Shamsi حوض شمسی, the great tank built by Sultan Iltutmish التتمش around 1230 near Mehrauli महरौली, lies half-dry in the summer of a late monsoon. Water-carriers, bhishtis بہشتی, come with their leather bags and leave empty. People offer the prayer for rain, salat al-istisqa صلاة الاستسقاء, in the open field. When the clouds finally break, the tank fills, and the city celebrates by the water with sweets.",
  "delhi-sultanate:flood": "Imagined: the Yamuna यमुना, swollen by monsoon, overflows the low quarters of old Delhi دہلی below the citadel. Families move to the higher ground near the Qutb with whatever they can carry. Firuz Shah Tughlaq فیروز شاه تغلق later built canals to tame these rivers, but in the earlier years there was only waiting. The Sufis of the khanqahs خانقاہیں open their doors to feed the displaced, as their masters taught them.",
  "delhi-sultanate:wind": "Imagined: in the hot months before the monsoon, the andhi आँधी sweeps over Delhi دہلی, a dust storm that turns noon to night. The muezzin's call is drowned by the wind. Merchants pull down the awnings in the bazaar, and horsemen shelter in the caravanserais. Some of the old minarets crack. When the wind is gone, the people sweep the dust from their courtyards and give thanks that nothing worse happened.",
  "delhi-sultanate:fire": "In 1398 Timur تیمور sacked Delhi دہلی. His troops looted and burned the city for days; tens of thousands of people were killed, and artisans were carried off to Samarkand سمرقند to build his mosques. The sultan Nasir ud-Din Mahmud ناصرالدین محمود fled. It was said that for two months afterward not a bird moved in the city. The Delhi Sultanate survived in name, but the old splendour of the city had gone up in smoke.",
  "delhi-sultanate:thunder": "Imagined: on a monsoon night in Delhi دہلی, a storm of thunder breaks over the dargah درگاہ of Nizamuddin Auliya نظام‌الدین اولیا. The qawwals قوال, mid-song in the courtyard, pause; then the leader laughs and starts again, louder, the harmonium straining to answer the sky. Amir Khusrau امیر خسرو, the saint's disciple, is said to have written a verse on the sound of rain. The pilgrims stay, soaked and singing.",
  "delhi-sultanate:birth": "Imagined: in a noble house of the sultanate, a son is born. The father whispers the call to prayer into his right ear, the adhan اذان, so that the first thing he hears is the name of God. On the seventh day, the aqiqa عقیقہ: his head is shaved, its weight in silver given to the poor, and a goat sacrificed. Will this child become a sultan's general, or a scholar? The family prays he will be just.",
  "delhi-sultanate:death": "Imagined: when a man of the sultanate dies, he is washed, wrapped in a white kafan کفن, and buried within the day, his face turned toward Mecca. The janaza جنازہ prayer is said in the mosque courtyard. Great men were buried in domed tombs; Iltutmish's tomb near the Qutb Minar قطب منار, built around 1235, is among the earliest. For the poor, a simple mound is enough. All return to God, it is said.",

  // ── Tughlaq Dynasty ──
  "tughlaq-dynasty:hunt": "Firuz Shah Tughlaq فیروز شاه تغلق, who ruled from 1351 to 1388, loved the hunt and built hunting lodges, shikargahs شکارگاہیں, in the ridge forests around Delhi دہلی; the ruined lodge called Kushk-i-Shikar کوشک شکار still stands on the Delhi Ridge. Imagined: the old sultan rides out at dawn with cheetahs and falcons, and his courtiers note with relief that he is in good humour; on hunting days, petitioners found him more generous.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:battle": "In the 1330s and 1340s Muhammad bin Tughlaq's empire, stretched from the Himalaya to Madurai மதுரை, begins to break apart. Revolt follows revolt: in Bengal, in Ma'bar معبر in the far south, in the Deccan where the Bahmanis rise in 1347. The sultan marches from one end of India to the other putting them down. In 1351 he dies of fever on campaign in Sindh, still chasing a rebel. The historian Badauni بدایونی wrote that the sultan was freed from his people, and they from him.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:gather": "Firuz Shah Tughlaq فیروز شاه تغلق dug canals from the Yamuna यमुना and the Sutlej to water the dry lands of Hisar हिसार, and his reign is remembered for cheap grain and orchards: some 1,200 gardens around Delhi دہلی, it was said. Imagined: villagers in Hisar who had never seen such green gather mangoes and grapes from the new orchards, and give thanks for a sultan who built rather than burned.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:ritual": "Imagined: in 1327, before moving the whole court and population of Delhi دہلی to Daulatabad دولت‌آباد in the Deccan, Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق prays istikhara استخاره and asks the scholars for counsel. He is learned in philosophy, astronomy and law. The scholars are cautious; some dare say nothing. The sultan rises from his prayer certain. History would judge the decision harshly, but that night, the palace was hushed and solemn, waiting on God.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:rain": "Imagined: during the great famine of the 1330s, when rains failed year after year across the Doab, Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق opened state granaries and gave loans to farmers to dig wells. The people of Delhi دہلی crowded the open prayer grounds for istisqa استسقاء, the prayer for rain. When the clouds came at last, children ran into the lanes and old men wept. Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة, then a judge in Delhi, saw the hunger and recorded it.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:flood": "Imagined: Firuz Shah's canals bring water to the desert, but in the year of a great monsoon the Yamuna यमुना bursts into them and floods the low villages near Hisar हिसार. Officials rush to close the sluice gates. Farmers who had begged for water now beg it to stop. In time the water recedes, leaving rich silt, and the farmers grumble and sow a double crop.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:wind": "Imagined: on the long road to Daulatabad دولت‌آباد in 1327, the people of Delhi دہلی, forced to move south by order of Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق, march for forty days. A dust storm hits the column in the Malwa plateau; tents fly away, the old and the sick fall behind. Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة later heard that many died on the road. When the sultan let them return years later, few did.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:fire": "Tughlaqabad تغلق‌آباد, the vast fortified city built by Ghiyas ud-Din Tughlaq غیاث‌الدین تغلق in the 1320s, was soon abandoned. Legend says the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya نظام‌الدین اولیا, angered that the sultan had forbidden workmen to build his stepwell, cursed it: may it be inhabited by herders, or stay desolate. The sultan died in 1325 when a pavilion collapsed on him. Shepherds later lit their fires in the empty halls.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:thunder": "Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق introduced copper and brass token coins in 1329 to stand for silver. Within months, every house became a mint, and forgeries flooded the markets. The sultan had to buy back the fakes with real silver, and heaps of copper coins lay outside the fort of Tughlaqabad تغلق‌آباد. The news struck Delhi دہلی like thunder; merchants said that the sultan's ideas were brilliant and his people were not ready.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:birth": "Imagined: in Delhi دہلی in the 1300s, a son is born to a Tughlaq noble. The adhan اذان is whispered in his ear and a Sufi from the khanqah خانقاہ of Nizamuddin نظام‌الدین is asked to bless him. Some in the family hope he will be a great general like Ghiyas ud-Din غیاث‌الدین; others whisper that the times are dangerous and the sultan's favour fickle. The mother only hopes he will live through his first summer.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:death": "Ghiyas ud-Din Tughlaq غیاث‌الدین تغلق lies in a red sandstone tomb, built in 1325, that once stood in an artificial lake connected to his fort at Tughlaqabad تغلق‌آباد. Imagined: on the day of his burial, the people of Delhi دہلی gather, the janaza جنازہ prayer is said, and the body is laid facing Mecca. Some mourn; some whisper about the collapsed pavilion and the saint's curse. His son, Muhammad, is now sultan.",

  // ── Burmese ──
  "burmese:hunt": "Imagined: in the teak forests of Upper Burma, the royal elephant catchers of the Konbaung ကုန်းဘောင် kings drive wild elephants into a stockade, the kheddah, with drums and shouts. A white elephant, if one is ever found, is a sign of a righteous king and is received with festivals at the court of Ava အင်းဝ or Mandalay မန္တလေး. The catchers themselves are proud men, risking their lives for the glory of the throne.",
  "burmese:battle": "In 1767, after a siege of more than a year, a Burmese army stormed and burned the great city of Ayutthaya อยุธยา. Among the thousands of captives marched north were dancers, singers and musicians. At the court of Ava အင်းဝ their music took root: Burmese musicians still play the Yodaya ယိုးဒယား songs, named for the fallen city, slow and graceful on the saung gauk စောင်းကောက် harp. A war's bitterest loss became, in time, a shared song.",
  "burmese:gather": "Imagined: in the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ valley, women gather thanaka သနပ်ခါး bark, grinding it on flat stones with water into a pale yellow paste they smear on their cheeks against the sun. On the river islands, families pick tamarind and beans as the waters fall. They carry their harvest in baskets slung from shoulder poles to the market, smiling under their thanaka.",
  "burmese:ritual": "Imagined: at the Shwedagon ရွှေတိဂုံ pagoda in Yangon ရန်ကုန်, a family asks a fortune-teller, a bedin saya ဗေဒင်ဆရာ, about a son's future. He consults the boy's birth day, for each day of the week has its own planet and animal, and his planetary post at the pagoda. The family pours water over the Buddha image at the corner of their day and lights candles. Gongs and bells sound across the platform. They leave hopeful.",
  "burmese:rain": "Thingyan သင်္ကြန်, the water festival of April, marks the Burmese New Year, in the hottest month just before the monsoon. For days, the whole country throws water at each other from buckets, hoses and silver bowls, laughing. It is meant to wash away the sins of the old year. The rains themselves follow soon, and the farmers plough their fields, already wet with festival joy.",
  "burmese:flood": "Imagined: on the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ delta, the monsoon swells the river until the paddies vanish under a brown sea. Villages on stilts become islands; children paddle to school in boats. The fishermen are busy and happy: the flood brings fish into the fields. The old monk in the village monastery says the river has always done this and always will.",
  "burmese:wind": "On 2 May 2008 Cyclone Nargis struck the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ delta with a storm surge that swept over the low villages at night. Around 140,000 people died. In the days after, monks from nearby monasteries and young volunteers from the towns loaded small boats with rice, water and tarpaulins and rowed out through the flooded paddies to find the survivors. The delta remembers the grief, and the small boats that came.",
  "burmese:fire": "Tazaungdaing တန်ဆောင်တိုင်, the festival of lights, falls on the full moon of the eighth Burmese month in November, at the end of the rainy season. Hot-air balloons of paper are launched into the night sky, especially at Taunggyi တောင်ကြီး in the Shan hills, some carrying fireworks that burst high overhead. Crowds cheer and duck when one misfires. Monks are given robes woven overnight in weaving contests. Fire here is joy.",
  "burmese:thunder": "Imagined: in Burmese tradition, the beginning of the rains is marked by Waso ဝါဆို, when monks begin their three-month retreat. The first thunderstorms crack over the plains around Bagan ပုဂံ, lighting up its thousands of temples. Villagers say the nats နတ်, the spirits, are restless. They offer coconuts and bananas at the spirit house and wait for the rain to settle the dust.",
  "burmese:birth": "Imagined: in a Burmese village, a baby is born, and the family notes the day of the week, for the child's name will begin with a letter belonging to that day. An astrologer is consulted for the naming ceremony. A Monday child is said to be jealous, a Thursday child mild. The grandparents wonder whether this child will one day become a monk, a farmer, or something no one has imagined.",
  "burmese:death": "In Burma the dead are given a funeral that is more about merit than mourning. The body is washed and dressed, and a coin is placed in the mouth to pay the ferryman. Monks chant the Abhidhamma အဘိဓမ္မာ, and the family offers food to the monks for seven days so the dead may be reborn well. When a revered monk dies, his body may be lain in state for a year, then cremated in a grand ceremony with rockets.",

  // ── Khmer ──
  "khmer:hunt": "Imagined, after the reliefs of Angkor អង្គរ and the Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan 周達觀, who lived there in 1296: in the forests around Angkor, men catch wild elephants and rhinoceros, and trap kingfishers whose feathers are sold to Chinese merchants. The king rides out on an elephant under a golden parasol. Hunters bring back deer and boar, but the people of Angkor ate mostly fish and rice.",
  "khmer:battle": "In 1177 a Cham fleet sailed up the Mekong មេគង្គ and into the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប and sacked Angkor អង្គរ. The Khmer prince who became Jayavarman VII ជ័យវរ្ម័នទី៧ drove them out in a great naval battle on the lake, carved on the walls of the Bayon បាយ័ន with boats, drowned warriors and crocodiles. He went on to build the walled city of Angkor Thom អង្គរធំ and more hospitals than any king before him. The Khmer remember him as a healer-king.",
  "khmer:gather": "Imagined: at the edges of the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប, as the water falls in the dry season, families gather fish trapped in the shallows, and women collect lotus roots and water lilies. The fish are salted and pounded into prahok ប្រហុក, the fermented paste that flavours nearly every Khmer meal. In the evening, the smell of drying fish hangs over the villages, and people sing as they work.",
  "khmer:ritual": "Imagined: at Angkor Wat អង្គរវត្ត, the brahmins of the king's court perform a rite before the rains, chanting Sanskrit verses and offering rice, flowers and fire to Vishnu វិស្ណុ. The king asks the court astrologers, the hora ហោរា, whether the year will bring good harvests. The answer is given in sacred terms. In the Royal Ploughing Ceremony still held today, the royal oxen choose among bowls of rice, beans and water to predict the season.",
  "khmer:rain": "Imagined: in the dry months, the moat of Angkor Wat អង្គរវត្ត shrinks and the rice fields crack. The Khmer kings built the great barays បារាយណ៍, reservoirs like the West Baray, eight kilometres long, to hold water. When the monsoon finally arrives in May, the barays fill, the rice is sown, and the people go to the temples to give thanks with offerings of fruit and flowers. The whole land breathes again.",
  "khmer:flood": "Each monsoon the Mekong មេគង្គ rises so high that the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប River reverses its flow, pushing water back up into the great lake, which swells to several times its size and floods the forest. In November, when the river turns back again, the Khmer celebrate Bon Om Touk បុណ្យអុំទូក, the water festival, with long-boat races in Phnom Penh ភ្នំពេញ, illuminated floats and fireworks. The flood is life here.",
  "khmer:wind": "Imagined: a typhoon blowing in from the South China Sea crosses the Annamite mountains and reaches the plains of Cambodia as a violent storm. The wooden houses of Angkor អង្គរ, unlike the stone temples, are shaken and some blown down. The people shelter in the temples' galleries. In the morning, the stones are unmoved, and the people rebuild their houses quickly, as they always have.",
  "khmer:fire": "Imagined: at Angkor អង្គរ, the cremation of a king was a vast spectacle, the pyre built high in the royal square before the Terrace of the Elephants. In the dry season, farmers also set fire to the stubble of the rice fields; at night, the plain around Angkor glowed red. The fire cleared the fields for new crops, and the ash fertilized the soil.",
  "khmer:thunder": "Imagined: at the beginning of the monsoon, thunder cracks over the towers of the Bayon បាយ័ន, where two hundred stone faces of the bodhisattva smile in every direction. The people in the city beneath say the naga នាគ, the serpent spirit of the waters, is rising. Children run to the moats to watch the first rain hit the water. The farmers smile: the thunder means the season has turned.",
  "khmer:birth": "Imagined: in an Angkorian house on stilts, a woman gives birth beside a fire, where she will lie for days to dry and warm her body, a custom Zhou Daguan 周達觀 noted in 1296. The midwife ties a red string around the baby's wrist. Monks bless the family. Will this child carve the stone faces of the Bayon បាយ័ន, or plough the king's fields, or rule? Only the gods know.",
  "khmer:death": "Zhou Daguan 周達觀, the Chinese envoy who lived at Angkor អង្គរ in 1296, wrote that the Khmer often left their dead in the open fields for vultures and dogs, and thought it a good sign if the body was taken quickly. Others were cremated. Today, Khmer Buddhist funerals have monks chanting, the body cremated at a pagoda, and the ashes kept in a stupa. Pchum Ben ភ្ជុំបិណ្ឌ, each autumn, feeds the hungry ghosts of the ancestors.",

  // ── Malay ──
  "malay:hunt": "Imagined: in the forests of the Malay Peninsula, a pawang ڤاوڠ, the village shaman-hunter, leads men after wild boar and mouse-deer, the kancil کنچيل, which in Malay tales outwits the tiger and the crocodile. Before entering, he asks permission of the forest spirits with a jampi جامڤي, a spoken charm. Tigers are not hunted lightly; people call the tiger Datuk داتوق, grandfather, to avoid offending it.",
  "malay:battle": "In 1511 a Portuguese fleet under Afonso de Albuquerque attacked Melaka ملاک, the richest port of Southeast Asia. Sultan Mahmud Shah's men fought from the bridge over the river and with war elephants, but after weeks the city fell. The sultan retreated and his son founded the sultanate of Johor جوهر. Malay tradition remembers Hang Tuah هڠ تواه, the legendary laksamana لقسامان of Melaka, who said: Takkan Melayu hilang di dunia تق کن ملايو هيلڠ د دنيا, the Malays will never vanish from the earth.",
  "malay:gather": "Imagined: on the coasts of the Straits of Melaka ملاک, Orang Laut اورڠ لاوت, the sea people, gather sea cucumbers, pearl shells and turtle eggs from the reefs, and the camphor and gaharu ݢاهارو wood that the forest people trade down the rivers. Merchants from China, India and Arabia wait in Melaka's harbour for the monsoon wind to turn. The gatherers know the price of each thing and bargain hard.",
  "malay:ritual": "Imagined: in a Malay village in the sultanate of Melaka ملاک, before a long voyage, the captain asks the imam to pray istikhara استخارة with him, and they hold a kenduri کندوري, a communal feast with prayers. An older bomoh بوموه may still quietly offer rice to the spirits of the sea. Islam and older custom sit side by side. The village wishes him well; he sails at the turn of the wind.",
  "malay:rain": "In the Malay world, life turns on the monsoon winds: the northeast monsoon from November brings heavy rain to the east coast of the Peninsula, and the southwest monsoon carries ships from India. Imagined: in Kelantan کلنتن, after the dry spell, the first heavy rains of the musim tengkujuh موسيم تڠکوجوه arrive, the fishermen haul their boats onto the beach, and families gather at home to eat and wait for months of rain.",
  "malay:flood": "Every year the northeast monsoon floods the rivers of Kelantan کلنتن, Terengganu and Pahang, and villagers call it bah. In December 2014 the bah kuning باه کونيڠ, the yellow flood, was the worst in memory, forcing hundreds of thousands from their homes. Malay houses on stilts, rumah panggung رومه ڤڠݢوڠ, were built for this. Neighbours share food from mosque kitchens, and when the water falls, everyone helps scrub the mud out.",
  "malay:wind": "Imagined: a storm called the Sumatras blows out of the west across the Straits of Melaka ملاک before dawn, a squall line of wind and lightning feared by sailors. A trading prahu ڤراهو caught in it must ride out the gale under bare masts. Its crew pray, and recite the azan اذان to calm the wind. By sunrise the sea is flat again, and they laugh and shake the water from their sarongs.",
  "malay:fire": "Imagined: in the early days of the Sultanate of Melaka ملاک, the houses of palm thatch and timber were close together, and fire spread quickly. The town was rebuilt many times. In the dry season, villagers clear land for rice by burning, the tebas-tebang, and the smoke hangs over the river valleys. Fire is a tool and a danger, and the people respect both.",
  "malay:thunder": "Imagined: in Malay belief, thunder and lightning are signs of the power of God, and a strong storm is a time to recite prayers. Children are told not to point at lightning, and mirrors are covered. When the thunder rolls over the rice fields, the old farmers smile: the rains will be good and the padi will grow tall. The fear and the hope come together.",
  "malay:birth": "Imagined: in a Malay kampung کامڤوڠ, a woman gives birth with the help of a mak bidan مق بيدن, the midwife. The father recites the azan اذان in the baby's right ear. Then comes the berpantang برڤنتڠ, forty-four days of rest for the mother with herbal baths and warmth. At the naming, the cukur jambul چوکور جمبول, a lock of the baby's hair is cut while the family sings praises of the Prophet. What will this child bring?",
  "malay:death": "When a Malay Muslim dies, the body is washed, wrapped in a white kain kapan کاين کاڤن and buried the same day if possible, facing Mecca. Men carry the bier to the mosque for the solat jenazah صلاة جنازه, then to the graveyard, where the imam recites the talqin تلقين, reminding the dead of the answers to give the angels. Neighbours bring food. On the third, seventh and hundredth days, tahlil تهليل prayers are said.",

  // ── Vietnamese ──
  "vietnamese:hunt": "Imagined: in the mountains of northern Vietnam, a Vietnamese hunter and his Mường guide track deer and wild boar with crossbows, the weapon the Lạc Việt (雒越) legend says the turtle god gave to King An Dương Vương (安陽王). They leave an offering of rice and wine at the edge of the forest for the mountain spirit, Sơn Tinh (山精). Tigers are feared and respected, called Ông, grandfather.",
  "vietnamese:battle": "Bạch Đằng (白藤) River, 1288. The Mongol fleet of Kublai Khan, retreating down the river, met the trap prepared by Trần Hưng Đạo (陳興道): iron-tipped stakes driven into the riverbed, hidden by the high tide. The Vietnamese lured the fleet in, the tide fell, the stakes pierced the hulls, and the Mongol ships were destroyed. It was the same trick Ngô Quyền (吳權) had used against the Southern Han in 938. The river is still a symbol of resistance.",
  "vietnamese:gather": "Imagined: in the Red River delta after the harvest, women and children wade the paddies gathering crabs, snails and small fish trapped in the mud, and pick water spinach from the canals. In the late afternoon, they carry their baskets home on bamboo poles, singing quan họ (官賀), the call-and-response folk songs of Bắc Ninh (北寧). The rice harvest is in; the mood is easy.",
  "vietnamese:ritual": "Imagined: on the eve of Tết (節), the lunar new year, a family in Hà Nội (河內) sets out offerings on the ancestral altar: a five-fruit tray, sticky rice cakes, bánh chưng (餅𥺊), incense and paper gold. They ask the ancestors for guidance and a good year. On the first morning, someone of good fortune is chosen to be the first to step through the door, the xông đất. At the temple, people draw fortune slips.",
  "vietnamese:rain": "Imagined: after the dry season, the farmers of the Mekong delta watch for the southwest monsoon of May. When the first rains come, the dust turns to mud, the canals fill, and the families rush out to plant. Children splash in the rain with banana leaves on their heads. The grandmother says that the rain is the gift of heaven and that the rice will grow tall this year.",
  "vietnamese:flood": "The legend of Sơn Tinh (山精) and Thủy Tinh (水精): the Mountain Spirit and the Water Spirit both sought the hand of the daughter of the Hùng King (雄王). Sơn Tinh arrived first with his gifts and won her, and the furious Water Spirit raised the floods, but the Mountain Spirit raised the hills higher. Every year, the Vietnamese say, Thủy Tinh still attacks with the monsoon floods of the Red River, and every year he is beaten back by the dikes.",
  "vietnamese:wind": "Typhoons, bão, strike the central coast of Vietnam almost every autumn. Imagined: in a fishing village near Huế, the men drag their basket boats, thúng chai, up the beach and tie down their roofs with ropes and stones. The family huddles together while the wind howls. In the morning, they walk out to see what is left, and begin, together, to rebuild.",
  "vietnamese:fire": "Imagined: in a village of the Red River delta, a family burns paper offerings, vàng mã, gold and silver paper money, paper clothes and houses, for their ancestors on the fifteenth day of the seventh lunar month, the Vu Lan (盂蘭) festival. The fire carries the offerings to the other world. Children watch the ash float up, and the parents tell them stories of the grandparents they never met.",
  "vietnamese:thunder": "Imagined: in Vietnamese folk belief, thunder is Thiên Lôi (天雷), the god of thunder, who strikes down wrongdoers with his axe. When the storm rumbles over the Red River delta, children who misbehaved are told that Thiên Lôi is watching. The adults laugh, but they still burn incense when the thunder is very loud. After the storm, the rice fields shine green.",
  "vietnamese:birth": "Imagined: in a Vietnamese family, the child is a link in a long line of ancestors. A month after birth, the family holds the đầy tháng, a ceremony to thank the twelve midwife goddesses, bà mụ, who taught the baby to smile and suck. Offerings are made and the child is formally introduced to the ancestors. Will this child be another Trần Hưng Đạo (陳興道), or simply a good son? They pray for both.",
  "vietnamese:death": "In a Vietnamese funeral, the family dresses in white mourning headbands. The body is laid in a coffin at home, with the ancestor altar, incense and a photograph. Monks or a master of ceremonies chant, and a funeral band of drums and the kèn plays. The family walks behind the coffin to the grave. Afterward, the dead joins the ancestors, honoured at the altar every year on the day of death, the giỗ.",

  // ── Lac Viet ──
  "lac-viet:hunt": "Imagined, after the Đông Sơn (東山) drums: around 500 BCE in the Red River valley, Lạc Việt (雒越) hunters in feathered headdresses pursue deer and wild birds with spears and crossbows. Their bronze drums show such men, and herons and deer in rings around a central sun. The hunters tattoo their bodies with water creatures, as Chinese chroniclers later noted, so the river dragons will spare them.",
  "lac-viet:battle": "In 40 CE two sisters, Trưng Trắc (徵側) and Trưng Nhị (徵貳), remembered together as Hai Bà Trưng (二婆徵), raised the Lạc lords of the Red River delta after Trưng Trắc's husband was executed by the Han governor. Riding elephants at the head of their followers, they took sixty-five citadels and held them for three years. In 43 CE the Han general Ma Yuan 馬援 crushed the rising, and the sisters are said to have drowned themselves in the Hát River. Their temples still hold festivals each spring.",
  "lac-viet:gather": "Imagined: in the delta of the Red River, around 300 BCE, Lạc Việt (雒越) women gather shellfish and snails from the marshes and harvest wild rice. Their villages stand on stilts. Men catch fish with bamboo traps. In the evenings, the people gather beneath the drum house to eat and to sing. The land is wet and generous, and they are content.",
  "lac-viet:ritual": "Imagined, after the Đông Sơn (東山) bronze drums: the Lạc lords beat the great drums in the rain-calling ritual, the frogs cast on the drum's rim signalling their power over rain. Priests in feathered costumes dance to the drumbeat, and boats carry warriors in a procession on the river. They ask the spirits whether the year will be kind. The thunder of the drum echoes over the paddies.",
  "lac-viet:rain": "Imagined: in the hot season before the rains, the Lạc Việt (雒越) beat their bronze drums, whose rims bear little frogs, the heralds of rain. The villagers wait for the clouds over the mountains of the north. When the first monsoon storm comes in, they shout and dance, and the drums answer the thunder. The rice planting begins with joy, as it has for centuries.",
  "lac-viet:flood": "The legend of Lạc Long Quân (貉龍君), the Dragon Lord of the waters, and Âu Cơ (嫗姬), the mountain fairy, whose hundred sons were born from a hundred eggs, gave the Việt their origin. Fifty went to the sea with their father, fifty to the mountains with their mother. Imagined: when the Red River floods, the Lạc people say the dragon father is visiting, and they move to higher ground until he returns to the sea.",
  "lac-viet:wind": "Imagined: in the delta of the Red River, around 200 BCE, a great storm comes in off the Gulf of Tonkin. The stilt houses shake and the canoes are dragged up the riverbank. The Lạc people shelter beneath the floor of the communal house, where the great bronze drum is kept. The storm passes; the drum is unharmed, and they take it as a good omen.",
  "lac-viet:fire": "Imagined: in the Đông Sơn (東山) villages around 300 BCE, bronze smiths pour molten metal into clay moulds to cast the great drums. The whole village gathers to watch. If the casting fails, it is a bad omen; if it succeeds, the drum is beaten for the first time, and the village celebrates for days. The smiths are honoured as masters of fire and earth.",
  "lac-viet:thunder": "Imagined: the Lạc Việt (雒越) believed that their bronze drums could summon rain and thunder. When the sky darkens over the Red River valley, the lord orders the great drum beaten, and the people say the drum and the thunder are speaking to each other. The children hide; the old men nod. In the legends of the Hùng Kings (雄王), the thunder god himself protects the land.",
  "lac-viet:birth": "Imagined, after the legend: the Việt trace their origin to a hundred eggs laid by Âu Cơ (嫗姬), from which a hundred sons hatched, the eldest becoming the first Hùng King (雄王) of Văn Lang (文郎). In a Lạc village, every birth recalls that story. The midwife washes the newborn in river water; the father ties a thread to its wrist. Will this child be a lord or a farmer? The ancestors will decide.",
  "lac-viet:death": "Imagined, after finds at Việt Khê and other Đông Sơn (東山) sites: a Lạc lord is laid in a boat-shaped coffin hollowed from a tree trunk, with bronze drums, weapons and jars for the journey. The coffin is buried beneath a mound by the river. Mourners in feathered headdresses dance and beat drums, as on the drum faces. The boat carries the dead to the land of the ancestors.",
  // ── Hindustanis: new activities ──
  "hindustanis:siege": "Kalinjar कालिंजर, May 1545. Sher Shah Suri شیر شاہ سوری had sat for months before the great hill fort in Bundelkhand बुंदेलखंड, its walls rising out of sheer rock. His men raised mounds and batteries; inside, the garrison watched their tanks of rainwater sink in the summer heat. On the day of the final assault a rocket struck the fort wall, rebounded into a heap of gunpowder near the commander, and he was carried out burning. The fort fell that evening. He died hours later, told of the victory.",
  "hindustanis:triumph": "In 1573 Akbar اکبر came back from his lightning march to Gujarat, nine days across desert and river. At his new city of Fatehpur Sikri फ़तेहपुर सीकरी the naqqar khana نقار خانہ, the drum house over the gate, thundered day and night; shehnai शहनाई players blew from the walls and the streets were hung with cloth. Later the great Buland Darwaza بلند دروازہ, the Lofty Gate, rose there in red sandstone. Under it ordinary people still pass, many not knowing what it once remembered.",
  "hindustanis:build": "Agra आगरा, from 1632. For some twenty years about twenty thousand workers built the tomb Shah Jahan شاہ جہاں raised for Mumtaz Mahal ممتاز محل: masons from Kannauj कन्नौज, inlayers who set carnelian and lapis into white Makrana मकराना marble, calligraphers, carpenters for the brick scaffolding. Elephants and bullock teams hauled blocks up a long earthen ramp. In the workshops along the Yamuna यमुना the hammers kept a steady rhythm, and the men sang to it. Their names are mostly lost; their work is the Taj Mahal تاج محل.",
  "hindustanis:voyage": "In 1585 the English traveller Ralph Fitch went down the Yamuna यमुना and the Ganga गंगा from Agra आगरा in a fleet of some one hundred and eighty boats laden with salt, opium, carpets and cloth. For five months the boats drifted past ghats and temples, Allahabad इलाहाबाद, Banaras बनारस, Patna. Boatmen sang as they rowed and poled, the steersman calling the rhythm. At every bend new towns appeared on the banks, until the river widened toward Bengal and the sea.",
  "hindustanis:festival": "In Braj ब्रज, the country of Krishna कृष्ण around Mathura मथुरा, Holi होली lasts for days. At Barsana बरसाना, Radha's village, women greet the men who come from Nandgaon नंदगाँव with long bamboo staves in the lathmar Holi लठमार होली; the men shelter under leather shields and sing teasing hori होरी songs. Then the colours fly: red gulal गुलाल, yellow, green, thrown by everyone at everyone. Caste and age blur under the powder. The dholak beats, bhang is passed round, and spring has arrived.",
  "hindustanis:games": "In the courtyard of Akbar's palace at Fatehpur Sikri फ़तेहपुर सीकरी, a vast cross-shaped board of chaupar चौपड़ is laid into the paving stones. Legend says the emperor played from a raised seat while young women in coloured dresses moved as living pieces at the call of the cowrie throws. In the villages the same game is played on cloth boards under neem trees, and in the akharas अखाड़े wrestlers in red earth train for kushti कुश्ती, rubbing mustard oil and clay into their shoulders.",
  "hindustanis:drought": "In 1783 the monsoon failed across the Doab दोआब and Hindustan हिंदुस्तान. The famine that followed is remembered as the Chalisa चालीसा, named for the year 1840 of the Vikram era. Wells ran dry, cattle died along the roads, and whole villages walked toward the cities looking for grain. In Lucknow लखनऊ the nawab Asaf-ud-Daula آصف الدولہ began building the great Imambara امام باڑہ, it is said, so that the hungry could earn their bread by working on it. Some laboured by day and some, too proud to be seen, by night.",
  "hindustanis:quake": "Delhi दिल्ली, 15 July 1720. A strong earthquake shook the city at night; walls cracked, houses fell, people ran out into the lanes. The chroniclers wrote that the shocks returned again and again for some forty days, so that families slept outside under the monsoon sky. The mosques and temples filled with prayer. Musicians of the court who had played all night fell silent, then, as the ground settled, slowly began to play again.",
  "hindustanis:winter": "Imagined, as in Premchand's story Poos ki Raat पूस की रात: in the month of Poos a poor farmer, Halku हल्कू, goes out to guard his wheat field against nilgai. He has only a thin blanket; the cold wind cuts through it. He curls up with his dog Jabra जबरा, lights a fire of dry leaves, and smokes his chillum. Around the plains the fog lies white over the mustard. The night is long; the cold is the real enemy of the poor.",
  "hindustanis:plague": "In 1616 a plague broke out in the Punjab and spread toward Agra आगरा and Delhi दिल्ली. The emperor Jahangir جہانگیر wrote in his memoir that before anyone fell ill, mice rushed out of their holes and died, so that people learned to leave their houses when they saw dead mice. Those struck had swellings under the arm and in the groin and died within days. Jahangir recorded it with puzzled sorrow: no one, he wrote, could remember such a sickness.",
  "hindustanis:wedding": "Imagined, in a town on the Ganga गंगा: the groom rides to the bride's house on a white mare, his face veiled by a sehra सेहरा of flowers, while the baraat बारात dances behind him to brass band and dhol. At the door the bride's sisters block the way and demand a fee. Late at night, as the shehnai शहनाई plays, the couple walk seven times around the sacred fire. At dawn comes the bidai बिदाई: the bride leaves her parents' home, and everyone weeps.",
  // ── Indians: new activities ──
  "indians:siege": "Golconda గోల్కొండ, 1687. Aurangzeb's army surrounded the great fort of the Qutb Shahi قطب شاہی kings in the Deccan for eight months. The monsoon came, the trenches flooded, and famine and sickness broke out in the Mughal camp; the defenders made sallies at night. In the end a gate was opened from within by a bribed officer. Yet one commander, Abdur Razzaq Lari, fought on with seventy wounds and lived. The diamond trade of Golconda had made it rich; the siege made it a ruin.",
  "indians:triumph": "In 1520 the Vijayanagara ವಿಜಯನಗರ king Krishnadevaraya ಕೃಷ್ಣದೇವರಾಯ won the battle of Raichur ರಾಯಚೂರು. The Portuguese visitor Domingo Paes then watched the Mahanavami ಮಹಾನವಮಿ festival in the capital: for nine days the king sat on a great stone platform while wrestlers fought, dancers whirled with jewelled anklets, and hundreds of horses and elephants passed in parade. Fireworks lit the night. Paes wrote that the city was as large as Rome and the celebration beyond anything he had seen.",
  "indians:build": "Ellora वेरूळ, 8th century. Under the Rashtrakuta राष्ट्रकूट king Krishna I, workers began to cut a whole temple out of a basalt hill, from the top down: the Kailasa कैलास, Shiva's mountain home. With chisels and hammers they removed some two hundred thousand tonnes of rock, leaving shrines, bridges, elephants and gods standing free in the stone. An inscription says the architect himself cried out in wonder: how did I make this? No mistake could be undone.",
  "indians:voyage": "The Qissa-i Sanjan قصهٔ سنجان tells how Zoroastrians, leaving Iran, sailed to the coast of Gujarat ગુજરાત many centuries ago. The local ruler sent them a bowl filled to the brim with milk: there is no room. Their priest stirred in a spoonful of sugar and sent it back: we will sweeten your land without spilling it. They were allowed to stay, keep their fire and their faith, and adopt the local language. The Parsis પારસી still tell the story.",
  "indians:festival": "In 643 the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang 玄奘 went to Prayag प्रयाग, where the Ganga गंगा meets the Yamuna यमुना, and saw the great assembly of King Harsha हर्ष. Every five years, he wrote, the king gave away everything in his treasury to monks, Brahmins, the poor and orphans, until he had only his clothes left, and then he borrowed a robe from his sister. Hundreds of thousands bathed at the sangam संगम. Today the Kumbh Mela कुम्भ मेला draws even more.",
  "indians:games": "Chaturanga चतुरङ्ग, the game of four limbs of an army, infantry, cavalry, elephants and chariots, appears in India by the 6th century and travelled west to Persia as chatrang چترنگ and on to become chess. A legend says a sage invented it to teach a proud king and asked as reward one grain of rice on the first square, doubled on each square after. The king laughed, then found his granaries could not pay. Around the board, players still laugh like that.",
  "indians:drought": "In 1630 the rains failed in the Deccan and Gujarat ગુજરાત. The court historian Abdul Hamid Lahori عبدالحمید لاهوری wrote that life was offered for a loaf but no one would buy, that rank was sold for a cake and no one cared. The English merchant Peter Mundy, travelling the roads, saw them lined with the dead. Shah Jahan شاہ جہاں opened soup kitchens in Burhanpur and Ahmedabad and remitted taxes. The famine stayed in folk memory for generations.",
  "indians:quake": "On 1 September 1803 an earthquake centred in the Garhwal गढ़वाल Himalaya shook northern India from the mountains to the plains. In the hills whole villages slid away and pilgrims on the road to Badrinath बद्रीनाथ were buried; in Mathura मथुरा temples cracked; in Delhi दिल्ली the top storey of the Qutb Minar قطب منار was thrown down. The mountain people said the gods of the peaks had moved. Later masons rebuilt what they could and the pilgrims came again.",
  "indians:winter": "In Kashmir कश्मीर the forty hardest days of winter are called chillai kalan چلہٕ کلان, from late December to the end of January. Snow closes the passes, the lakes freeze at the edges, and the valley lives on dried vegetables and stored rice. Under the long woollen pheran پھیرن each person carries a kangri کانگڑ, a little clay pot of embers in a wicker basket, held against the chest. Families sit together, tell stories and drink salt pink noon chai نون چائے until spring.",
  "indians:plague": "Imagined: when smallpox comes to a village of the plains, people do not say the child is ill but that the Mother has come. They worship Sitala शीतला, the Cool One, who rides a donkey with a broom and a pot of water. Neem leaves are hung over the door and laid on the bed; no cooking with fire is allowed in the house. Women sing her songs softly, begging her to be gentle and to go away.",
  "indians:wedding": "In February 1633 Shah Jahan شاہ جہاں married his eldest son Dara Shikoh دارا شکوہ to Nadira Banu Begum نادرہ بانو بیگم in Agra आगरा. The English traveller Peter Mundy watched the fireworks over the Yamuna यमुना and the processions: elephants, horses, dancers, the bride's dowry carried through the streets. The cost was said to be more than three million rupees. The henna night was sung by women of the harem. The couple's love was real; they stayed together until their end.",
  // ── Bengalis: new activities ──
  "bengalis:siege": "In the 1740s the Maratha horsemen called bargis বর্গি raided western Bengal year after year. Villagers fled across the rivers or shut themselves behind the mud walls of a zamindar's compound, watching the dust of horses on the horizon, burying their brass pots and rice. Mothers still sing the lullaby born then: the child sleeps, the village is quiet, the bargis have come to the land; the birds have eaten the paddy, how will I pay the tax?",
  "bengalis:triumph": "Imagined: on Bijoya Dashami বিজয়া দশমী, the last day of Durga Puja দুর্গাপূজা, the goddess has won her battle with the buffalo demon Mahishasura মহিষাসুর. Married women smear vermilion on each other in sindur khela সিঁদুর খেলা, the dhakis ঢাকি beat their great drums with feather plumes, and the clay image is carried to the river and immersed. Then everyone embraces, elders bless the young, sweets are passed round: Shubho Bijoya শুভ বিজয়া. Victory and farewell taste the same.",
  "bengalis:build": "In the late 8th century the Pala পাল king Dharmapala ধর্মপাল built the Somapura Mahavihara সোমপুর মহাবিহার at Paharpur পাহাড়পুর: a vast square monastery of one hundred and seventy-seven cells around a towering cruciform shrine. Brick-makers fired millions of bricks from Bengal clay, and potters made thousands of terracotta plaques showing monkeys, musicians, farmers and gods. Imagined: as they worked, the masons sang in the old tongue that would become Bengali, the same tongue as the Buddhist Charyapada চর্যাপদ songs.",
  "bengalis:voyage": "In the Manasamangal মনসামঙ্গল poems the merchant Chand Sadagar চাঁদ সদাগর sails from Champaknagar চম্পকনগর with seven great ships, the madhukar মধুকর fleet, down the Bengal rivers toward the sea and distant ports. The serpent goddess Manasa মনসা, whom he refuses to worship, sinks his ships one by one in storms. Boatmen of the delta still sing bhatiali ভাটিয়ালি on the long reaches of the river, drawn-out and lonely, a voice over the water calling for the far bank.",
  "bengalis:festival": "Pohela Boishakh পহেলা বৈশাখ, the first day of the Bengali year in mid-April. Shopkeepers open a new red ledger, the halkhata হালখাতা, and offer customers sweets as old debts are settled. People wear white and red, eat panta bhat পান্তা ভাত, soaked rice, with fried hilsa, and gather at village fairs with clay toys, bamboo flutes and swings. The baul বাউল singers come with their ektaras একতারা, and the songs say: let the old year go, let the new one be kind.",
  "bengalis:games": "When the rivers are full after the monsoon, the villages of Bengal hold nouka baich নৌকা বাইচ, boat races. Long thin boats, carved and painted, carry dozens of rowers who pull in time to the beat of a drum and the call of a leader standing at the stern. The banks are crowded; whole villages shout for their boat. The songs of the race are called sari gaan সারি গান, and their rhythm is the paddle stroke itself.",
  "bengalis:drought": "In 1769 the rains failed across Bengal and Bihar. The famine of 1770, remembered as chhiyattorer monnontor ছিয়াত্তরের মন্বন্তর, the famine of the year seventy-six in the Bengali era, killed perhaps a third of the people of Bengal. Families sold their children, ate seed grain and leaves, and walked to the towns to die. Fields went back to jungle and tigers came into empty villages. The tax collectors, it was remembered bitterly, kept collecting.",
  "bengalis:quake": "On 2 April 1762 a great earthquake struck the coast of Arakan and Chittagong চট্টগ্রাম. Parts of the coast rose out of the sea; elsewhere land sank and was swallowed, and a surge ran up the rivers. In Dhaka ঢাকা the rivers rose suddenly and boats were tossed onto the banks, and many people drowned. Boatmen said the river had heaved like an animal. Afterwards the villages mourned, and new mud islands, chars চর, appeared where water had been.",
  "bengalis:plague": "In 19th-century Bengal, when cholera came with the hot season, villagers turned to Ola Bibi ওলাবিবি, the Lady of the Flux, worshipped by Hindus and Muslims alike. Her small shrines stood at the edges of villages, and offerings of rice and milk were left there. Singers told her story, and people tied threads and vowed gifts. When the epidemic passed they thanked her together, whatever their faith, for sparing the children.",
  "bengalis:wedding": "Imagined: a Bengali wedding begins with gaye holud গায়ে হলুদ, when turmeric paste is smeared on bride and groom in their own homes and women sing. In the evening the bride is carried on a low wooden seat by her brothers, hiding her face behind two betel leaves, until the moment of shubho drishti শুভদৃষ্টি, the first auspicious look. Conches are blown, women ululate, ulu ulu, and red and white shankha-pola শাঁখা-পলা bangles mark her new life.",
  // ── Dravidians: new activities ──
  "dravidians:siege": "A poem of the Purananuru புறநானூறு records how the Chola சோழர் Nalankilli நலங்கிள்ளி besieged his own kinsman Nedunkilli நெடுங்கிள்ளி in the fort of Uraiyur உறையூர். Inside, the elephants bellowed for fodder, children cried for milk, and women had no water to cook. Then the poet Kovur Kilar கோவூர் கிழார் stood between the two and sang: the one who wins is a Chola, the one who loses is a Chola; whoever falls, your own house falls. A poem against a siege, kept for two thousand years.",
  "dravidians:triumph": "Around 1023 the armies of Rajendra Chola இராசேந்திர சோழன் returned from a long march north to the Ganga. They brought back pots of Ganga water. The king took the title Gangaikonda கங்கைகொண்டான், the one who took the Ganga, built a new capital, Gangaikonda Cholapuram கங்கைகொண்ட சோழபுரம், and poured the holy water into a vast tank, the Cholagangam சோழகங்கம், which an inscription calls a liquid pillar of victory. The nadaswaram நாதஸ்வரம் and thavil தவில் played as the water was poured and the people bathed.",
  "dravidians:build": "Thanjavur தஞ்சாவூர், 1010. Rajaraja Chola இராசராச சோழன் completed the Brihadisvara temple, its tower rising about sixty-six metres, the tallest in India at the time. Granite was brought from quarries far away, since none lies nearby. Tradition says the great capstone was dragged up a ramp several kilometres long from a village still called Sarapallam சாரப்பள்ளம், the scaffold hollow. On the walls Rajaraja had the names of hundreds of dancers, musicians and lamp-keepers carved, with their wages.",
  "dravidians:voyage": "From the 9th to the 13th century Tamil merchant guilds such as the Ainnurruvar ஐந்நூற்றுவர், the Five Hundred, sailed from the Coromandel coast with the monsoon winds to Sri Lanka, Sumatra, Java and the ports of Southeast Asia, and even to China. Their inscriptions in Tamil are found in Barus in Sumatra and in Quanzhou. Imagined: on deck, sailors sing to keep the rhythm of the ropes, scanning the horizon for the green line of the islands.",
  "dravidians:festival": "Pongal பொங்கல், in mid-January, is the harvest festival of the Tamil country. Kolam கோலம் patterns of rice flour are drawn before every door at dawn. In new clay pots the new rice is boiled with milk and jaggery in the open courtyard, and when it froths over the rim everyone shouts: Pongalo pongal பொங்கலோ பொங்கல்! On the next day, Mattu Pongal மாட்டுப் பொங்கல், the cattle are washed, their horns painted, and garlands hung around their necks, for they helped bring in the harvest.",
  "dravidians:games": "The Sangam poems already sing of eru thazhuvuthal ஏறு தழுவுதல், embracing the bull: young herdsmen of the mullai முல்லை lands leapt onto charging bulls to win a bride. Today jallikattu ஜல்லிக்கட்டு is still held at Pongal பொங்கல் around Madurai மதுரை. Bulls burst out of the vadivasal வாடிவாசல் gate one by one; young men grab the hump and hold on as long as they can. The crowd roars, the drums play, and the bull's owner is as proud as the winner.",
  "dravidians:drought": "An old commentary on the grammar of Iraiyanar இறையனார் tells that once, in the Pandya பாண்டியர் country, a famine lasted twelve years. The king could no longer support the poets of his academy, the sangam சங்கம், and sent them away to live wherever they could. When the rains returned he called them back, but the old grammar had been forgotten. Whether history or legend, it remembers that when the rain fails, even songs are scattered.",
  "dravidians:quake": "Imagined, after the epic Manimekalai மணிமேகலை: Puhar புகார், the great Chola சோழர் port at the mouth of the Kaveri காவிரி, is said to have been swallowed by the sea because its king forgot to hold the festival of Indra இந்திரன். The ground shook, the waves came in, and the streets of merchants and dancers vanished. Divers today have found ruins off the coast of Poompuhar பூம்புகார். The poets wrote it as warning: a city that forgets its festivals may lose its shore.",
  "dravidians:plague": "In the Tamil villages smallpox was the visit of Mariamman மாரியம்மன், the mother goddess of rain and fever. When the pox came, people tied bunches of neem leaves over their doors and laid the sick on neem leaves. During the month of Aadi ஆடி her temples overflow: women carry pots of rice porridge, kool கூழ், on their heads, and some walk on burning coals to fulfil vows. The goddess who burns can also cool.",
  "dravidians:wedding": "The Sangam poem Akananuru அகநானூறு 86 describes an ancient Tamil wedding: a pandal of fresh sand, a bright lamp, garlands hung, and the bride bathed by four married women who poured water with flowers and blessed her to be a good wife. Today the high point is the tying of the thali தாலி: as the groom ties the gold thread, the nadaswaram நாதஸ்வரம் and thavil தவில் burst into the kettimelam கெட்டிமேளம், a loud drumroll that drowns out any ill-omened word.",
  // ── Gurjaras: new activities ──
  "gurjaras:siege": "Chittorgarh चित्तौड़गढ़, 1567 to 1568. The great fort of Mewar मेवाड़ on its long hill was besieged by Akbar for four months. The defenders, led by Jaimal जयमल and Patta पत्ता, held as mines were dug and walls blown open. When the end came, the women of the fort, according to tradition, chose jauhar जौहर, the fire, rather than capture, and the men went out to fight in saffron. Akbar later set up statues of Jaimal and Patta on elephants at the gate of Agra fort.",
  "gurjaras:triumph": "Imagined: in a village of Marwar मारवाड़ the men come home from a long campaign. The women stand at the edge of the village with brass pots on their heads and sing the old welcome: kesariya balam केसरिया बालम, aavo ni, padharo mhare des पधारो म्हारे देस, saffron beloved, come, come to my land. The Manganiyar मांगणियार singers play the kamaicha कमायचा; the drums answer. Lamps are lit, and the returned are given milk and jaggery before they step inside.",
  "gurjaras:build": "Patan પાટણ, around 1063. Queen Udayamati ઉદયમતી built a stepwell, the Rani ki Vav રાણકી વાવ, in memory of her husband Bhimadeva ભીમદેવ. Seven storeys of steps lead down to the water through galleries carved with some five hundred sculptures of gods, dancers and nymphs. In the dry land of Gujarat ગુજરાત, water is holy and a well is a temple. Later the river flooded and filled it with silt, and it slept underground for centuries until it was dug out.",
  "gurjaras:voyage": "From Cambay ખંભાત, Surat સુરત and Diu, Gujarati merchant ships sailed with the monsoon to Hormuz, Aden, Malacca and the ports of Java. The Portuguese apothecary Tomé Pires wrote in 1512 that Cambay stretched out two arms, one to Aden and one to Malacca. The ships carried cotton cloth, indigo and beads, and returned with spices and gold. On the shore families waited for months, singing for the sails to appear.",
  "gurjaras:festival": "Navratri નવરાત્રી, the nine nights of the goddess, is the great festival of Gujarat ગુજરાત. In every lane and village square a clay pot with holes, the garbo ગરબો, holds a lamp; around it women and men dance garba ગરબા, clapping as they turn in a ring. Later the dandiya raas દાંડિયા રાસ begins, with pairs of painted sticks clacking in rhythm. The dancing lasts until dawn, nine nights in a row, in mirrorwork skirts and embroidered cloth.",
  "gurjaras:games": "On Uttarayan ઉત્તરાયણ, in mid-January, the sky over Gujarat ગુજરાત fills with kites. From every rooftop people fly paper kites on manja માંજો, string coated with glue and ground glass, and try to cut each other's lines. When a kite is cut, the winner shouts Kai po che કાઇ પો છે! and the children race through the lanes to catch the fallen one. Families eat undhiyu ઊંધિયું and sesame sweets on the roof all day, and at night they fly lanterns.",
  "gurjaras:drought": "The famine of 1899 to 1900, the Vikram year 1956, is remembered in Gujarat ગુજરાત and Rajasthan राजस्थान as the Chhappaniyo Dukal છપ્પનિયો દુકાળ, the famine of fifty-six. The rains failed completely; cattle died first, then people. Folk songs still speak of it, and in Gujarati, chhappaniyo has become a word for any terrible hunger. Old people would warn children who wasted food: you would not do that if you had seen the fifty-six.",
  "gurjaras:quake": "Kutch કચ્છ, 16 June 1819. A great earthquake shook the Rann of Kutch કચ્છનું રણ. The fort and town of Sindri sank and the sea rushed in to cover it, while a few miles away a long ridge of earth rose up, which people named Allah Bund, the dam of God. Bhuj ભુજ, the capital, was badly damaged and many died. The salt flats and their herders lived with the new shape of the land afterward.",
  "gurjaras:winter": "Imagined: in the Thar थार desert, days are mild but winter nights are bitterly cold, the sand frosted by dawn. A Rabari રબારી herder sleeps among his camels and sheep, wrapped in a thick woollen dhabla ધાબળો, a small fire of thorny khejri खेजड़ी wood at his feet. Before sleeping he plays the algoza अलगोज़ा, two flutes at once, and his son sings. At first light he warms his hands on a cup of camel milk tea.",
  "gurjaras:plague": "In 1815 plague broke out in Kutch કચ્છ and spread through Kathiawar કાઠિયાવાડ for several years. Villages emptied as people fled to the fields. Imagined: on Shitala Satam શીતળા સાતમ, the seventh day in the month of Shravan શ્રાવણ, women did not light their stoves but ate food cooked the day before, cold, to honour Shitala Ma શીતળા મા, the goddess who cools fevers. They prayed for their children, and the bards later sang of the dead.",
  "gurjaras:wedding": "Imagined: a Gujarati wedding. The groom arrives with his procession; at the door the bride's mother greets him with a lamp and tries to grab his nose, reminding him to be humble. Under the mandap મંડપ the couple walk four times around the fire. Meanwhile the two families sing fatana ફટાણાં, teasing songs that mock each other's relatives, and everyone laughs. Then the women bring the dhol out, and garba ગરબા begins.",
  // ── Delhi Sultanate: new activities ──
  "delhi-sultanate:siege": "In 1303 a Mongol army under Targhi came suddenly to Delhi دہلی and camped around the city for some two months. Alauddin Khalji علاءالدین خلجی dug trenches around his camp at Siri سیری and waited. Inside the city, grain, water and fodder ran short; the chronicler Barani برنی wrote that people could not go out even to fetch firewood. Then, one day, the Mongols simply rode away. In thanks, it is said, Alauddin made Siri into a new walled city.",
  "delhi-sultanate:triumph": "The chronicler Barani برنی wrote that when good news came to Delhi دہلی in the Khalji days, wooden pavilions called qubbas قبہ were raised in the streets, hung with brocade and lit with lamps. Musicians and dancers performed in them for days; sherbet was given out free. Amir Khusrau امیر خسرو wrote long poems on each campaign, praising the swords and the horses, but the people simply enjoyed the music, the lights and the sweets.",
  "delhi-sultanate:build": "Around 1295 Alauddin Khalji علاءالدین خلجی dug the great tank of Hauz-i Alai حوض علائی near Siri سیری, a reservoir of over fifty hectares to hold the monsoon rains for the city. Thousands of labourers dug earth and carried it in baskets on their heads to build the banks, while masons faced them with stone. Later it silted up and Firuz Shah cleaned it again and built a college on its shore. Today it is called Hauz Khas حوض خاص, the royal tank.",
  "delhi-sultanate:voyage": "In the 13th century, as the Mongols swept across Central Asia, scholars, poets and craftsmen fled south over the mountains and came to Delhi دہلی. Among them was a Turkish officer, Saifuddin سیف‌الدین, from near Samarkand سمرقند. In 1253, at Patiyali पटियाली on the Ganga, his son was born: Amir Khusrau امیر خسرو, who would write in Persian and Hindavi हिंदवी and mix their music. Refugees and their children made Delhi a city of many tongues.",
  "delhi-sultanate:festival": "Tradition says that the Sufi saint Nizamuddin Auliya نظام‌الدین اولیا was grieving the death of his young nephew. His disciple Amir Khusrau امیر خسرو saw village women going to a temple in yellow, carrying mustard flowers for Basant बसंत, the spring festival. He put on yellow, took mustard flowers, and danced singing before the saint, who smiled for the first time in months. Ever since, Basant has been celebrated at the dargah درگاہ with yellow and qawwali قوالی.",
  "delhi-sultanate:games": "In 1210 at Lahore لاہور, Qutbuddin Aibak قطب‌الدین ایبک, the first sultan of Delhi دہلی, was playing chaugan چوگان, polo, when his horse fell and he was crushed under it. He died there. Chaugan was the great game of the court: riders in silk swinging long sticks after a ball across a dusty field, drums beating at each goal. Even after Aibak's death the sultans kept playing; the game was too much loved to give up.",
  "delhi-sultanate:drought": "During the reign of Jalaluddin Khalji جلال‌الدین خلجی in the 1290s, the rains failed around Delhi دہلی. The chronicler Barani برنی wrote that grain rose to a jital جیتل a ser, and that poor people came into the city from the villages in such hunger that some, in groups, threw themselves into the Yamuna यमुना and drowned. Imagined: in the khanqah خانقاہ of the Sufis, the langar لنگر kitchen gave out bread until there was none left.",
  "delhi-sultanate:quake": "On 6 July 1505 a great earthquake shook Agra, where the Lodi sultan Sikandar سکندر had recently founded his new city. Chroniclers wrote that the mountains trembled and lofty buildings fell, and that thousands of people were buried. Sikandar Lodi had the city rebuilt. Imagined: survivors told of the noise that came first, like an army of horsemen under the ground, and then the cracking walls.",
  "delhi-sultanate:winter": "Imagined: in January a thick fog lies over Delhi دہلی and the Yamuna यमुना. In the courtyard of Nizamuddin Auliya's khanqah خانقاہ the poor wrap themselves in shawls while the langar لنگر serves hot khichri खिचड़ी and bread. Qawwals sit by a brazier and sing Khusrau's verses in Hindavi हिंदवी. The saint himself, it is said, fasted often and gave away all that came to him, so the cold would be lighter for others.",
  "delhi-sultanate:plague": "Imagined: when an epidemic struck Delhi دہلی in the hot months, people tied amulets around the necks of their children and went to the tombs of saints to pray. Hakims of the Unani یونانی tradition prescribed rosewater and cooling drinks and burned sandalwood to cleanse the air. The dead were buried quickly. In the evening the qawwals قوال sang at the dargah درگاہ, and the crowd, frightened and grieving, sang with them.",
  "delhi-sultanate:wedding": "A song attributed to Amir Khusrau امیر خسرو is still sung at weddings: kahe ko byahi bides काहे को ब्याही बिदेस, why did you marry me away to a far land, father? The bride sings that she is like a bird of his courtyard, who flies away at dawn. Imagined: the henna night, the nikah نکاح before the qazi قاضی, the procession with drums, and then this song at the bidai बिदाई, when even the hard-hearted cry.",
  // ── Tughlaq Dynasty: new activities ──
  "tughlaq-dynasty:siege": "Delhi دہلی, December 1398. Timur's army defeated the sultan's forces outside the city, and the last Tughlaq ruler, Nasiruddin Mahmud ناصرالدین محمود, fled by night. The people of Delhi shut themselves inside the walls of Siri, Jahanpanah جہاں پناہ and old Delhi, hoping to be spared. When the soldiers entered, quarrels over grain and goods turned into days of looting and killing. Survivors were marched away as captives, masons among them. A chronicler wrote that for two months not a bird moved in the city.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:triumph": "Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة described how Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق returned to Delhi دہلی after a journey. Wooden pavilions, several storeys high and hung with silk, were raised along the road; in them sat musicians and dancing girls. Small catapults were mounted on elephants, and from them gold and silver coins were flung into the crowd as the sultan rode by. People scrambled, laughed and sang. Ibn Battuta wrote that no ruler he knew gave away money so freely.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:build": "In 1356 Firuz Shah Tughlaq فیروز شاه تغلق decided to bring an ancient stone pillar of Ashoka 𑀅𑀲𑁄𑀓 / अशोक from Topra टोपरा to Delhi دہلی. The chronicler Afif عفیف tells how it was wrapped in reed and raw silk cotton, lowered onto a cart with forty-two wheels, and pulled by some eight thousand four hundred men, then floated down the Yamuna यमुना on great boats. In Delhi it was raised on top of a palace, Firuz Shah Kotla فیروز شاه کوٹلہ, where it still stands, its old inscription then unreadable to all.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:voyage": "In 1327 Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق ordered the people of Delhi دہلی to move to a new capital, Daulatabad دولت‌آباد, in the Deccan, some forty days' journey south. A road was built, shade trees planted, and rest houses set at each stage. Even so, many old and weak died on the way. Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة later heard that the city had been left almost empty. Years later most were allowed to return, and Delhi slowly filled again with voices.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:festival": "Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة described Eid عید in Delhi دہلی under the Tughlaqs. On the morning of the festival the sultan rode out on an elephant decked in gold, surrounded by banners and naqqara نقارہ drums, to the prayer ground outside the city. After prayers a great throne of gold was set up in the hall, and incense burned in huge censers. People in new clothes visited each other, sweets were exchanged, and the drums of the naubat نوبت played all day.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:games": "Imagined: in an open ground outside Delhi دہلی, the pahlwans پہلوان, wrestlers, wrestle before a crowd. They have rubbed their bodies with oil and earth and bowed to their teachers. The drums beat a rising rhythm as they grapple. A young wrestler throws a famous older one, and the crowd roars. The champion wins a turban and a purse, and his teacher embraces him. The ring is dug again with fresh earth for the next bout.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:drought": "From about 1335, for several years, the rains failed around Delhi دہلی. Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة, then a judge in the city, saw people eating hides and the flesh of dead animals, and saw women grinding bark. Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق ordered six months' grain given to every resident of Delhi, measured out at the gates, and dug wells and offered loans for seed. Still many died, and many villages emptied as their people walked away to find food.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:quake": "Imagined: one night in the dry season the ground under Delhi دہلی shakes. In the narrow lanes walls lean and lamps fall; in the courtyards of Firuz Shah's Kotla the ancient pillar sways but holds. People run out into the open, murmuring prayers. In the morning the masons go round to inspect cracks. The old say the earth has shrugged its shoulders, and the city gets back to work.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:winter": "On his way to Delhi دہلی, Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة crossed the Hindu Kush هندوکش in winter. He wrote that the name meant slayer of Indians, because so many slaves and servants brought from India died there of cold and snow. His party travelled at night, laying felt in front of the camels so they would not sink in the snow. When they came down at last to the plains, the warmth of India felt to them like a gift.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:plague": "Around 1334 Muhammad bin Tughlaq محمد بن تغلق marched south toward Ma'bar معبر, the far south, to put down a revolt. At Warangal వరంగల్ in Telangana a pestilence broke out in his army: soldiers sickened and died within days. Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة wrote that a great part of the army perished, and that the sultan himself fell ill. The march was abandoned. Imagined: the survivors walked home quietly, carrying only their memories of friends left in shallow graves.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:wedding": "Ibn Battuta ابن بطوطة was asked to help arrange the marriage of the sultan's sister to Amir Ghada, a prince from Syria. He describes the great pavilion of silk set up in the palace, the night of the henna, when the groom's hands were dyed, the musicians and singers, and trays of betel leaves and sweets carried round. Imagined: as dawn came, the women sang the last songs and the guests, tired and happy, went home through quiet streets.",
  // ── Burmese: new activities ──
  "burmese:siege": "Imagined: in a walled town on the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ, the gates are closed and the moat filled. Inside, the people store rice in the monasteries, and monks lead the chanting of parittas ပရိတ်, protective verses, every evening. Children carry water up to the walls; old women weave mats and cook for the defenders. Each night the gong sounds the watches. At last, after weeks, the besiegers' camp is seen burning, and they are gone at dawn.",
  "burmese:triumph": "Tradition says that in 1057 King Anawrahta အနော်ရထာ of Pagan ပုဂံ returned from Thaton သထုံ in the south with the sacred Pali scriptures of Theravada Buddhism, carried on thirty-two white elephants, together with monks, craftsmen and musicians. The people of Pagan lined the road with flowers and banners; drums and gongs played. Within decades thousands of pagodas rose across the plain. The victory remembered is less the battle than the books.",
  "burmese:build": "In 1790 King Bodawpaya ဘိုးတော်ဘုရား began building the Mingun မင်းကွန်း pagoda on the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ, meant to be the largest in the world, about one hundred and fifty metres high. Thousands of workers carried bricks; the work went on for over twenty years. He also had a giant bronze bell cast, the Mingun bell, weighing about ninety tonnes. After his death the work stopped, and an earthquake in 1839 split the huge brick base, which still stands, cracked.",
  "burmese:voyage": "Around 1180 the monk Chapata ဆပဒ of Pagan ပုဂံ sailed to Sri Lanka to study at the Mahavihara monastery in Anuradhapura අනුරාධපුරය. He stayed ten years, was ordained in the Sinhalese tradition, and returned with four other monks. Imagined: on the voyage home, the ship rides the monsoon winds across the Bay of Bengal, the monks chanting at dawn and dusk. When the green coast of the delta appears, they kneel on the deck.",
  "burmese:festival": "Thingyan သင်္ကြန်, the water festival, marks the Burmese New Year in April, in the hottest season. For three or four days everyone throws water at everyone: from bowls, buckets, hoses, and the scented water of thabyay သပြေ leaves. Young people sing and dance on decorated pavilions, the pandals. Old people wash Buddha images and the hair of elders, and give food to monks. On New Year's Day all is quiet, cleansed and forgiven.",
  "burmese:games": "Chinlone ချင်းလုံး is the Burmese game of the rattan ball. Six players stand in a circle and keep the ball in the air with feet, knees and heads, never the hands, passing it in graceful movements while a small ensemble plays. There is no winner and no loser; it is about beauty and the flow of the ball. At pagoda festivals, solo players perform tricks, balancing the ball on their heels behind their backs.",
  "burmese:drought": "Imagined: in the dry zone of central Burma, when the rains are late, the villagers hold a lun hswe လွန်ဆွဲ, a tug-of-war to call the rain. The men pull on one side, representing the rain spirits; the women pull on the other. The rain side must win. Children splash water on the ropes, and old men pour water at the nat shrine. When the clouds come, the whole village runs into the rain.",
  "burmese:quake": "On 23 March 1839 a great earthquake struck the royal cities of Ava အင်းဝ and Amarapura အမရပူရ near the Irrawaddy ဧရာဝတီ. The walls of the palace collapsed, pagodas cracked and fell, and many people were killed. The giant Mingun မင်းကွန်း pagoda was torn apart. Imagined: as the ground shook, monks rang the temple bells, and afterward villagers gathered the fallen brick and began to rebuild the shrines first, before their own houses.",
  "burmese:plague": "Imagined: when an epidemic comes, monks in saffron robes walk through the village chanting the Ratana Sutta ရတနသုတ်, the Jewel Discourse. Buddhists tell that the Buddha first taught it at Vesali ဝေသာလီ, a city stricken by disease, famine and evil spirits, and as his disciple chanted it and sprinkled water, the plague left. Families gather on their porches with lamps, sprinkling water and repeating the verses after the monks.",
  "burmese:wedding": "Imagined: at a Burmese wedding, the couple sit side by side before a respected elder. He joins their hands and dips them together into a silver bowl of water, then wraps them in white cloth, binding their lives together. A conch is blown; Brahmin priests, in the old court style, sing blessings. The couple bow three times to their parents and to monks. Then the hsaing waing ဆိုင်းဝိုင်း orchestra plays and the guests eat mohinga မုန့်ဟင်းခါး and tea leaf salad.",
  // ── Khmer: new activities ──
  "khmer:siege": "In 1177 a Cham fleet sailed up the Mekong មេគង្គ and across the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប lake and attacked Yasodharapura យសោធរបុរៈ, the Khmer capital. The city was taken by surprise, sacked and burned, and its king killed. For four years the land was in chaos. Imagined: the people of the villages along the lake fled into the forests, hiding their bronze images and their children, waiting for the foreign boats to go away.",
  "khmer:triumph": "In 1181 Jayavarman VII ជ័យវរ្ម័នទី៧ drove out the Cham forces in a great naval battle on the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប, the battle later carved on the walls of the Bayon បាយ័ន: war canoes, fishes, crocodiles eating the fallen. He was crowned king and built a new walled city, Angkor Thom អង្គរធំ, and on the site of his victory the temple of Preah Khan ព្រះខ័ន, the sacred sword. The people celebrated with music, dances and offerings at every shrine.",
  "khmer:build": "Angkor Wat អង្គរវត្ត was built in the first half of the 12th century under Suryavarman II សូរ្យវរ្ម័នទី២. Sandstone blocks were quarried in the Kulen គូលែន hills some forty kilometres away and floated along canals on rafts, then dragged into place by elephants and men. Thousands worked for decades: quarrymen, carvers of apsaras អប្សរា and battle scenes, builders of moats. Imagined: in the evening the workers rest on the causeway, and someone plays a flute.",
  "khmer:voyage": "In 1296 the Chinese envoy Zhou Daguan 周達觀 sailed from Wenzhou to Angkor អង្គរ. His ship rode the northeast wind down the coast, entered the Mekong មេគង្គ, and went up to the great lake of Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប. He wrote that he saw countless fish, villages on stilts, and finally the golden towers of the city. He stayed a year and described everything: markets, weddings, festivals, baths. Without him much of Khmer daily life would be forgotten.",
  "khmer:festival": "Choul Chnam Thmey ចូលឆ្នាំថ្មី, the Khmer New Year, comes in mid-April at the end of the harvest. People clean their houses, build sand stupas in the temple grounds, and bring food to monks. Water mixed with perfume is poured over Buddha images and over the hands of elders, who give blessings. In the villages young people play the old games: bas angkunh បោះអង្គុញ, throwing seeds, and chol chhoung ចោលឈូង, tossing a knotted scarf, with singing.",
  "khmer:games": "On the outer gallery of the Bayon បាយ័ន, carved around 1200, ordinary Khmer life appears: two men wrestle, cocks fight while their owners crouch beside them, boars face each other, and people play a board game. Zhou Daguan 周達觀 wrote of the festival season with boat races and fireworks. During Bon Om Touk បុណ្យអុំទូក, the water festival when the Tonle Sap ទន្លេសាប reverses its flow, long boats still race on the river to drums and shouts.",
  "khmer:drought": "Tree rings from the forests of Southeast Asia show that in the 14th and early 15th centuries long droughts alternated with violent monsoons around Angkor អង្គរ. The great reservoirs, the barays បារាយណ៍, dried and silted, and the canals broke. Imagined: farmers dug the mud of the moats for their rice seedlings, and families moved south to the rivers. The city of a million was slowly left to the trees.",
  "khmer:quake": "Imagined: earthquakes are rare in the Khmer lands, so when the ground shudders one night near Angkor អង្គរ, people say the naga នាគ, the great serpent beneath the earth, is turning in his sleep. Women light incense before the spirit house and the monks chant. In the morning the village elders walk around the temple to see if any stone has shifted. The sandstone, set without mortar, has moved only a little.",
  "khmer:plague": "Jayavarman VII ជ័យវរ្ម័នទី៧ built some one hundred and two hospitals, arogyasala អារោគ្យសាលា, across his realm, each staffed with doctors, nurses, cooks and pharmacists and supplied with honey, ghee and medicines. Their stone inscriptions say: the suffering of his people was his own suffering, more than his own body's pain. Imagined: in an epidemic, villagers bring the sick to the hospital chapel of the healing Buddha, and pray there.",
  "khmer:wedding": "Imagined: a Khmer wedding lasts for days. On the first, the groom's procession brings fruit, cakes and betel to the bride's house in trays. Later comes the hair-cutting, kat sak កាត់សក់, when relatives snip the couple's hair to cleanse them for new life, while the pinpeat ពិណពាទ្យ plays. At the knot-tying, sompeas ptem សំពះផ្ទឹម, guests tie red threads around the couple's wrists with blessings, and throw palm flower petals.",
  // ── Malay: new activities ──
  "malay:siege": "Malacca ملاک, July and August 1511. A Portuguese fleet anchored before the great trading port on the strait. For weeks the townspeople, merchants from Gujarat, Java and China among them, watched cannon fire across the river and the fighting for the bridge at its mouth. War elephants charged; the stockades burned. When the city fell, many families fled up the river and into the forests with what they could carry. The port that had welcomed every ship became a fortress.",
  "malay:triumph": "Imagined: a fleet of perahu ڤراهو returns to the river mouth of a Malay port after a long expedition. On the bank the royal nobat نوبت plays: the great drums nehara and gendang, the serunai سروناي shawm and the long silver nafiri نفيري trumpet, music heard only at royal moments. Families crowd the jetty; women wave scarves; children dive into the water to swim to the boats. That night there is a feast of rice, fish and kuih کوايه.",
  "malay:build": "Imagined: when a family builds a new house in a Malay kampung کامڤوڠ, the whole village comes to help in gotong-royong ݢوتوڠ رويوڠ. The posts of hardwood are raised on stones, the frame fitted with wooden pegs, without nails, the roof thatched with nipah palm. The tukang توکڠ, the master carpenter, chooses the right day and recites a doa دعا for the first post. Women cook for the workers, and when the roof is on, everyone eats together.",
  "malay:voyage": "Some fifteen hundred years ago, sailors from the islands of the Malay world crossed the whole Indian Ocean in outrigger canoes and settled Madagascar, off Africa. The Malagasy language is still close to a language of Borneo, and the outrigger canoe and rice terraces came with them. No one knows how many boats were lost. Imagined: after weeks at sea, a child at the bow cries out at the sight of an unknown green shore.",
  "malay:festival": "Hari Raya Aidilfitri هاري راي عيدالفطري ends the fasting month of Ramadan رمضان. Families return to their home kampung کامڤوڠ, balik kampung باليق کامڤوڠ, sometimes travelling for days. The night before, oil lamps called pelita ڤليتا are lit around the houses. In the morning, after prayer, children kneel before their parents and say maaf zahir dan batin معاف ظاهر دان باطن, forgive me in body and soul. Then come ketupat کتوڤت, rice in woven palm-leaf packets, rendang رندڠ, and the visiting of every house.",
  "malay:games": "Sepak raga سيڤق راݢ, kicking a ball woven from rattan, is an old Malay game; the Malay Annals already mention it at the court of Malacca ملاک. Players stand in a circle and keep the ball in the air with the feet, knees and shoulders. After the harvest the men fly the great wau واو, moon kites of Kelantan کلنتن, with long tails and a humming bow that sings in the wind. The best kite is the one that hums loudest.",
  "malay:drought": "Imagined: in the musim kemarau موسيم کمارو, the long dry season, the padi fields crack and the wells grow muddy. The villagers gather in an open field, led by the imam, for solat istisqa صلاة استسقاء, the prayer for rain. They turn their cloaks inside out as a sign that they ask for things to change. Children whisper that the sky is listening. In the evening, the first clouds come in from the sea.",
  "malay:quake": "In April 1815 Mount Tambora on the island of Sumbawa erupted in the largest eruption in recorded history. The explosions were heard more than a thousand kilometres away, in Sumatra; some thought it was cannon fire. The small kingdom of Tambora was buried, and its language lost. Ash darkened the sky for days, and the crops failed in the islands around. The next year, half a world away, Europe had a year without summer.",
  "malay:plague": "Imagined: when sickness spreads in a Malay village, people hold mandi Safar مندي صفر, the ritual bath in the month of Safar to wash away misfortune, and the bomoh بوموه, the healer, makes offerings at the river mouth to send the sickness away on a little boat of banana stems, loaded with rice and flowers, to drift out to sea. Imams read Yasin يٰسٓ in the surau سوراو. Each family keeps their children close.",
  "malay:wedding": "At a Malay wedding the bride and groom are raja sehari راج سهاري, king and queen for a day. First, at the berinai برينائي ceremony, their hands are stained with henna. On the wedding day the groom's procession arrives with the beat of kompang کومڤڠ frame drums and pencak silat ڤنچق سيلت dancers. The couple sit side by side on the pelamin ڤلامين, a decorated dais, in bersanding برسنديڠ, while relatives sprinkle them with scented rice and flowers. Guests take home bunga telur بوڠا تلور, decorated eggs.",
  // ── Vietnamese: new activities ──
  "vietnamese:siege": "Imagined: a Vietnamese village is surrounded by a thick hedge of thorny bamboo, the lũy tre, with only one gate. When bandits come in a time of troubles, the villagers close the gate and stand inside with spears and cudgels. Drums of the đình (亭), the communal house, beat the alarm. The old women hide the rice in jars buried in the fields. For days they hold out, until the raiders tire and go away.",
  "vietnamese:triumph": "A legend tells that Lê Lợi (黎利) received a magic sword from the waters to drive out invaders in the 15th century. After the victory, while he was boating on a lake in Thăng Long (昇龍), a golden turtle rose up and asked for the sword back. Lê Lợi gave it, and the turtle sank with it into the depths. The lake was named Hoàn Kiếm (還劍), lake of the returned sword. The people rejoiced, and the sword rested.",
  "vietnamese:build": "Imagined: along the Red River, villages have built and kept up the dykes for a thousand years. In the dry season, whole villages turn out: men dig earth and carry it in baskets slung on bamboo poles; women pack it with wooden rammers. A leader calls a work chant, hò dô ta, hò dô ta, and everyone answers on each blow. The dyke is the village's life; without it, the flood takes everything.",
  "vietnamese:voyage": "Imagined: in the 17th and 18th centuries, families from the poor central coast walked south with their buffaloes, tools and ancestral tablets to the wet plains of the Mekong delta. They cleared mangroves, dug canals, and faced crocodiles and tigers. A folk saying went: leaving home, you herd ducks you meet on the way. They built new đình (亭), sang vọng cổ around the fire, and missed the hills of home.",
  "vietnamese:festival": "Tết Nguyên Đán (節元旦), the lunar new year, is the greatest Vietnamese festival. A week before, families send the kitchen gods to heaven riding on carp to report on the household. Women wrap bánh chưng (餅𥺊), square cakes of sticky rice, mung bean and pork, in green dong leaves and boil them all night. Peach blossoms in the north and yellow apricot in the south fill the houses. The first visitor of the year brings luck.",
  "vietnamese:games": "In spring, after Tết (節), village festivals bring games. In the courtyard of the đình (亭), young men wrestle, vật, in loincloths, circling and grappling while drums beat. Others play đá cầu, kicking a shuttlecock of feathers with the side of the foot, or swing on bamboo swings. There are tug-of-war, cock fights and human chess, with young people in costumes as the pieces, and old men directing the game.",
  "vietnamese:drought": "Imagined: in the dry months, the paddies crack and the rice wilts. Two women stand on the edge of a canal, swinging a woven basket on ropes between them, gàu sòng, to lift water into the field. They swing in rhythm and sing: tát nước đầu đình (亭), scooping water by the communal house. Their arms ache, but they go on until the field shines with water again.",
  "vietnamese:quake": "Imagined, after the old court annals: the chroniclers of Đại Việt (大越) carefully recorded earthquakes in Thăng Long (昇龍), for a trembling of the earth was read as heaven's warning. The king would leave his throne room, reduce taxes, free prisoners and order prayers. In the city the people ran into the streets; the bells of the pagodas rang. When the earth was still again, they returned home, uneasy.",
  "vietnamese:plague": "In 1820 cholera reached Vietnam and spread from the south to the north. The court annals record that hundreds of thousands died in a few months. Emperor Minh Mạng (明命) ordered medicine distributed and ceremonies held to pray for the people. Imagined: in the villages the đình (亭) was filled with incense; coffins ran short; families buried their dead quickly and kept lamps burning all night for the souls.",
  "vietnamese:wedding": "A Vietnamese wedding begins with betel and areca, trầu cau, symbol of love, from an old tale of two brothers and a wife who turned into a limestone rock, an areca tree and a betel vine. At the engagement, the groom's family brings round red lacquer trays of betel, cakes, tea and wine. On the wedding day the couple bow before the ancestral altar. Then everyone eats and sings quan họ (官賀) love songs.",
  // ── Lac Viet: new activities ──
  "lac-viet:siege": "Imagined, after the legend of Cổ Loa (古螺): the king An Dương Vương (安陽王) holds his spiral citadel with a magic crossbow made from the claw of the golden turtle; no enemy can take it. But his daughter Mỵ Châu (媚珠) marries Trọng Thủy (仲始), son of the enemy, who steals the trigger. When the army attacks, the crossbow fails. She flees with her father, dropping goose feathers to mark the way, not knowing it guides her husband to them.",
  "lac-viet:triumph": "The legend of Thánh Gióng (扶董天王): a boy of three who had never spoken hears that invaders are coming and asks for an iron horse, an iron armour and an iron staff. He grows into a giant, rides into battle, and when his staff breaks he pulls up bamboo clumps to strike the enemy. After victory he rides up Sóc Sơn (朔山) mountain and ascends to heaven. Each spring the Phù Đổng (扶董) festival celebrates him with drums.",
  "lac-viet:build": "Imagined, after the legend of Cổ Loa (古螺): An Dương Vương (安陽王) orders a great citadel built in a spiral, but every night the walls collapse. Then a golden turtle, Kim Quy (金龜), rises from the river and shows him how to build it, and tells him the walls are undone by an evil spirit. The people work by torchlight to the beating of bronze drums, and the walls stand. The earthen rings are still there today.",
  "lac-viet:voyage": "Imagined, after the legend: Lạc Long Quân (貉龍君), a dragon lord of the sea, and Âu Cơ (嫗姬), a fairy of the mountains, had a hundred sons. But dragon and fairy could not live together. So fifty sons followed their father down to the sea, and fifty followed their mother up into the mountains. Each group set out in sorrow, promising to help the other in need. All the Việt are said to come from them.",
  "lac-viet:festival": "Imagined, after the scenes on the Đông Sơn (東山) drums: at the end of the harvest, people of the Lạc villages gather in a circle around the drum. Dancers wear headdresses of long bird feathers and carry axes and shields. Men pound rice in long wooden troughs in rhythm; others play khèn mouth organs. Boats with feathered rowers race on the river. At the centre of the drum shines a star, the sun.",
  "lac-viet:games": "Imagined, after the boat scenes on the Đông Sơn (東山) drums: on the Red River, long dugout canoes with bird-headed prows race each other, crewed by warriors in feather headdresses. A drummer stands in the middle; another man steers at the stern. People cheer on the banks; the winners get a jar of rice wine. Such races were once to honour the water spirits, and still are held in Vietnamese river villages.",
  "lac-viet:drought": "Imagined: when the rain does not come to the Red River plain, the Lạc lord brings out the great bronze drum. On its face stand small bronze frogs, for frogs call the rain. The drum is beaten day and night, its deep voice sent to the sky. Women pour water on the drum and dance; men sacrifice a buffalo. At last thunder answers from the mountains, and the people say the drum has spoken.",
  "lac-viet:quake": "Imagined: one night the hills around the Lạc villages shake, and stones roll down to the river. People run out of their stilt houses, clutching children. The shaman says the great dragon of the earth is turning, angry that the offerings were too few. In the morning the villagers bring rice, pigs and wine to the shrine at the foot of the mountain, and beat the bronze drum to calm it.",
  "lac-viet:plague": "Imagined: in the wet season a fever spreads through the Lạc villages of the low plain. The shaman dances with bells and burns herbs; families hang branches of thorny plants over their doors to block evil spirits. The sick are given broth of ginger and leaves. The dead are placed in boat coffins and buried near the river. Some families move up into the hills, where the air is cooler.",
  "lac-viet:wedding": "The legend of Sơn Tinh (山精) and Thủy Tinh (水精): the last Hùng King's daughter, Mỵ Nương (媚娘), had two suitors, the mountain spirit and the water spirit. The king asked for wedding gifts: an elephant with nine tusks, a rooster with nine spurs, a horse with nine manes. Sơn Tinh came first and took the bride. Imagined: in Lạc villages weddings still bring gifts of betel, sticky rice, pork, sung over to the drum.",

  // ── 楽 showcase: histories of each people's music ──
  "hindustanis:showcase": "North Indian music traces its roots to the chanted Sama Veda (सामवेद) and to the Natya Shastra (नाट्यशास्त्र), the early treatise that describes veenas, flutes and drums. Over the centuries temple and court singers refined raga and tala, and by the 15th and 16th centuries dhrupad (ध्रुपद) had taken shape, opening with a slow alap sung on syllables like nom and tom. Raja Man Singh Tomar of Gwalior (ग्वालियर) is remembered as a patron, and Tansen (तानसेन) sang at Akbar's court. The rudra veena (रुद्र वीणा) and pakhawaj (पखावज) were dhrupad's companions, while sarangi (सारंगी), bansuri (बांसुरी) and shehnai (शहनाई) sounded in other settings. Dhrupad singers and a few been players keep the tradition alive today.",
  "indians:showcase": "The Urdu (اردو) musical world grew where Persian and Indian sound met, at the courts of Delhi and Lucknow (لکھنؤ). Mughal court musicians joined Persian poetry to raga; the khayal (خیال) song form rose in the 18th century, shaped at Muhammad Shah's court by Niamat Khan, known as Sadarang. The ghazal (غزل), a Persian love lyric, found new life in Urdu verse, and Sufi qawwali (قوالی) filled the shrines. The sitar (ستار) took its familiar form in the 18th and 19th centuries, and the tabla (طبلہ) became the drum of the salons, with the sarangi (سارنگی) following the singer. Shehnai (شہنائی) and naqqara kettle drums sounded at gates and celebrations. Ghazal, qawwali and khayal are still sung and loved.",
  "bengalis:showcase": "Bengal's music begins in song. The Charyapada (চর্যাপদ), Buddhist mystic verses from roughly the 8th to 12th centuries, are each headed with the name of a raga. Jayadeva (জয়দেব) wrote the Gita Govinda, later sung in temples across India. From the 16th century the followers of Chaitanya (চৈতন্য) spread kirtan (কীর্তন), devotional singing with khol drums and kartal cymbals. Wandering Bauls (বাউল) sang of the divine within, to the ektara (একতারা), dotara (দোতারা) and khamak, and Lalon Fakir (লালন ফকির) became their best-loved poet. Boatmen sang the long, open bhatiali (ভাটিয়ালি) on the rivers to the bansi flute. Rabindranath Tagore (রবীন্দ্রনাথ ঠাকুর) wrote more than two thousand songs, often with the esraj. The dhak (ঢাক) still drums for Durga Puja.",
  "dravidians:showcase": "Tamil Sangam (சங்கம்) poems, from roughly the first centuries CE, already name the yazh (யாழ்) harp, flutes, drums and pann (பண்), the melodic modes. The epic Silappatikaram (சிலப்பதிகாரம்) describes dancers, musicians and their tunings in detail. From the 7th to 9th centuries the Tevaram (தேவாரம்) hymns were sung in Shiva temples, and oduvars (ஓதுவார்) still chant them. Temple processions came to be led by the nadaswaram (நாதஸ்வரம்) and thavil drum. In the 18th and 19th centuries Tyagaraja (త్యాగరాజు), Muthuswami Dikshitar and Syama Sastri composed the kritis at the heart of Carnatic music, sung with the veena (வீணை), venu flute and mridangam (மிருதங்கம்), joined in the 19th century by the violin. Carnatic concerts and temple music thrive today.",
  "gurjaras:showcase": "In Gujarat and Rajasthan music lives with hereditary families and devotional poets. A raga still carries the Gurjara name, Gurjari Todi (गुर्जरी तोड़ी). The 15th-century poet Narsinh Mehta (નરસિંહ મહેતા) wrote bhajans still sung in Gujarat, and Mirabai (मीराबाई) sang her songs to Krishna. The Manganiyar (मांगणियार) and Langa (लंगा) musicians served patron families for generations, singing genealogies, wedding songs and desert ballads to the kamaicha (कमायचा) and the Sindhi sarangi, with algoza (अलगोजा) twin flutes and dholak. Bhopa singers perform the epic of Pabuji (पाबूजी) before a painted scroll, bowing the ravanahatha (रावणहत्था), while Devnarayan's bhopas play the jantar zither. Nagara drums sound at festivals, and these musicians still play at weddings, fairs and festivals.",
  "delhi-sultanate:showcase": "From the 13th century, Persian-speaking courts in Delhi brought the ney, rabab (رباب), chang and naqqara (نقاره) of the Islamic world into north India. Amir Khusrau (امیر خسرو), poet and musician at the Delhi courts around 1300 and disciple of the Sufi Nizamuddin Auliya (نظام الدین اولیاء), is traditionally credited with shaping qawwali (قوالی) and with new ragas and forms, though many of these attributions are legendary. In Sufi gatherings, sama (سماع), song was used to move listeners toward ecstasy. Court naubat (نوبت) bands of surnay, karnay and naqqara marked the hours. Chronicles name the instruments and court customs, but the sound itself is lost, so this piece is a reconstruction. Qawwali, still sung at Nizamuddin's shrine in Delhi, is its living heir.",
  "tughlaq-dynasty:showcase": "From the 14th century the Persianate courts of the Deccan, first the Bahmanis (بہمنی) and later Bijapur, Golconda and Ahmadnagar, drew poets and musicians from Persia, Central Asia and India. Dakhni (دکنی), the Deccan's form of Urdu, became a language of song. Around 1600 Ibrahim Adil Shah II (ابراہیم عادل شاہ ثانی) of Bijapur wrote the Kitab-i-Nauras (کتاب نورس), a book of songs in Dakhni set to ragas, praising Saraswati and Sufi saints alike. Deccani painters made ragamala pictures and showed musicians with lutes, harps and drums. The kamancheh, chang, qanun and naqqara here follow Persian court practice; how Deccani ensembles actually sounded is not known, so this piece is a reconstruction of that meeting of Persian and Indian music.",
  "burmese:showcase": "In 802 the Pyu (ပျူ) city-states sent musicians to the Tang court, and Chinese records list their instruments, among them an arched harp. Under Pagan (ပုဂံ), temple murals and inscriptions show harps, drums and gongs. The saung gauk (စောင်းကောက်) arched harp became the instrument of the court and of the classical song repertory, the Mahagita (မဟာဂီတ). Outdoors the hsaing waing (ဆိုင်းဝိုင်း) ensemble formed around the pat waing (ပတ်ဝိုင်း), a circle of tuned drums, with the kyi waing gong circle, the hne (နှဲ) oboe and cymbals, accompanying pagoda festivals, nat spirit ceremonies, marionette theatre and zat pwe drama. The pattala bamboo xylophone and mi gyaung crocodile zither joined indoor music. Hsaing waing and the saung are still played today.",
  "khmer:showcase": "The reliefs of Angkor (អង្គរ), carved from the 9th to the 13th centuries, show musicians with arched harps, gongs, drums, conch shells and cymbals beside apsara dancers, and inscriptions record musicians and dancers given to temples. After Angkor the court pinpeat (ពិណពាទ្យ) ensemble took shape: roneat (រនាត) xylophones, the kong vong (គងវង់) gong circle, the sralai (ស្រឡៃ) oboe and skor thom drums, playing for court dance, shadow theatre and ceremonies. Mahori ensembles with the tro (ទ្រ) fiddle, krapeu crocodile zither and khloy flute played for entertainment, and the chapei (ចាប៉ី) lute accompanied sung storytelling. In the late 20th century many musicians died; survivors taught the young, and pinpeat again accompanies the royal ballet and temple festivals.",
  "malay:showcase": "Malay (ملايو) music grew along trade routes. Bronze drums and gongs reached the peninsula and islands in ancient times. The nobat (نوبت), the royal ensemble of nengkara kettle drum, gendang drums, serunai (سروناي) shawm and nafiri (نفيري) silver trumpet, came with Islamic courts; the Malay Annals (سجاره ملايو) describe it at Melaka (ملاک), and it still sounds at royal installations. The rebab (رباب) leads Mak Yong (مق يوڠ), the old dance-drama of the east coast. Gamelan Melayu, with saron and gongs, passed from the Riau-Lingga court to Pahang and Terengganu. The gambus lute came with Arab traders, and the biola violin arrived in the Portuguese era. Dikir barat choruses, kecapi zithers and seruling flutes are still heard today.",
  "vietnamese:showcase": "Việt music grew from village song and court ceremony. Court music took formal shape at the Lê court in the 15th century, and Nhã nhạc (雅樂), the court music later refined at Huế under the Nguyễn, survived into the 20th century. Ca trù (歌籌), sung by women to the long-necked đàn đáy lute and phách clappers, flourished in northern communal houses. In the north, quan họ (官賀) duets were traded between village groups at spring festivals. The đàn tranh zither, đàn nguyệt moon lute, tỳ bà and đàn nhị fiddle share roots with the wider East Asian world, while the đàn bầu monochord, with its bending glides, is first recorded in the 18th century and is now the emblem. Sáo trúc flute, trống drums and the temple chuông still sound.",
  "lac-viet:showcase": "The music of the Lạc Việt (雒越) is known only from bronze and stone. The Đông Sơn (東山) bronze drums, trống đồng, cast from around the 5th century BCE, are engraved with dancers in feathered headdresses, people pounding rice, and figures who seem to play mouth organs and drums; the Ngọc Lũ drum is the finest. The drums themselves were struck at ceremonies and treasured as heirlooms. Stone lithophones, đàn đá, found in the Central Highlands may be very old. No melodies survive, so this piece is a reconstruction: the khèn mouth organ, t'rưng bamboo xylophone, đàn goong tube zither, k'ní and cồng chiêng gongs come from the living traditions of the Central Highlands and the uplands, where gong music is still played at village festivals.",
});
